return [
    'EIR and Revenue Recognition' => [

        'How the EIR module fits together' => [
            'body' => '<p>The effective interest rate (EIR) is the rate that exactly discounts a loan\'s expected cash flows, including the fees that are part of making the loan, back to the amount MAIIC actually advanced. IFRS 9 requires interest income to be recognised at that rate rather than at the rate printed on the offer letter. The EIR module takes the platform from the bank\'s data to a locked EIR for every loan, a monthly amortised-cost roll-forward built on it, and an explanation of how the interest it calculates differs from what the general ledger (GL) posted.</p>'
                . '<h4>Where the screens are</h4><p>In the sidebar, under the <b>Financial modelling</b> heading, open <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b>. It holds six entries. EIR Data and EIR Calculations each open a row of tabs, one tab per screen; the other four are one screen each, and a screen with several views shows them as its own tabs:</p><ul>'
                . '<li><b>EIR Data</b>: <b>Contract Master</b>, <b>Cash Flows</b>, <b>Drawdowns</b>, <b>Reference Rates</b>, <b>Trial Balances</b>, <b>Write-offs</b> and <b>Schedule Review</b>.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Restructures &amp; Resets</b>: tabs <b>Restructure register</b>, <b>IFRS 9 note</b> and <b>Rate resets</b>.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Accounting Rules</b>: the rulebook.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Fee Classification</b>: tabs <b>Fee lines</b>, <b>Held GL fee lines</b>, <b>Source differences</b> and <b>Fees against the GL</b>.</li>'
                . '<li><b>EIR Calculations</b>: <b>Calculations</b>, <b>Blockers</b>, <b>Coverage by Portfolio</b>, <b>Largest Facilities</b> and <b>EIR as at a Date</b>.</li>'
                . '<li><b>GL Reconciliation</b>: tabs <b>Variance bridge</b> and <b>Facilities</b>.</li></ul>'
                . '<p>A screen has one row of tabs at most, under the breadcrumb, and the open tab is kept in the address, so a reload or a shared link opens the same tab. The import screen, <b>EIR Data Intake</b>, has no menu entry of its own: it opens from the <b>EIR Data Intake</b> button on the EIR Data tabs and from the import buttons on Drawdowns, Reference Rates and Fee Classification. The <b>Governance Centre</b>, where the calculation conventions are kept, is under <b>Administration &amp; support</b>, <b>Governance</b>.</p>'
                . '<h4>Who can see what</h4><ul><li><b>eir.view</b> opens the EIR Data tabs, Coverage (Blockers, Coverage by Portfolio, Largest Facilities), EIR as at a Date and GL Reconciliation.</li><li><b>settings</b> opens Accounting Rules, Fee Classification, Calculations and EIR Data Intake, and is needed to generate or approve a schedule and to run the revenue from GL Reconciliation.</li><li><b>eir.govern</b> is needed to upload a trial balance, to propose or approve a write-off, a restructure or a rate reset, to tie a held GL fee line to its loan, and to open the Governance Centre.</li><li><b>eir.export</b> is needed for the Excel and PDF downloads of GL Reconciliation.</li><li><b>eir.fees.import</b> alone opens EIR Data Intake on the fee type only, and Fee Classification to read.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>The pipeline, in order</h4><ol><li><b>Data.</b> The contract master (Extract A), the cash flows, the drawdowns, the prime lending rate series, the trial balance and the fees are loaded, mostly from the E-Banker pack and EIR Data Intake.</li><li><b>Write-offs, restructures and resets.</b> Write-offs the ledger carries are recognised automatically; others are proposed and approved in the register. Changes of terms are found as candidates and confirmed by two people.</li><li><b>Fee rules and classification.</b> The accounting rulebook suggests whether a fee is integral to the EIR; every fee line is classified by one person and reviewed by another.</li><li><b>Schedule review.</b> The original repayment schedule (version 1) is generated and approved by a second person on Schedule Review.</li><li><b>Readiness.</b> Before a contract can be solved its terms, schedule and fees are checked. A contract is ready or blocked, and every blocker has a code and a plain-English message (Blockers tab).</li><li><b>Calculation and lock.</b> Calculations solves the rate; a second person approves and locks it.</li><li><b>Monthly revenue.</b> The month-end run rolls each locked loan forward one month: opening balance, interest at the EIR, cash received, closing balance.</li><li><b>Reconciliation and reporting.</b> GL Reconciliation sets the calculated interest against the ledger and names the cause of each difference; EIR as at a Date gives the book at any date.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>Maker and checker in plain words</h4><p>A maker is the person who does a piece of work; a checker is the second person who confirms it. The platform enforces the split at every step that changes a figure: "A classifier cannot review their own decision." on fees, "The calculator cannot approve and lock their own EIR." on rates, "The person who prepared the schedule cannot approve it" on schedules, and "The person who proposed ... cannot approve it" on write-offs, restructures, rate resets and governance changes. An administrator may override only where the Governance Centre setting <b>Administrator override of maker-checker</b> is <b>Allowed for administrators</b> (the default is <b>Not allowed</b>), and every override is written to the audit log with a flag.</p>'
                . '<h4>Locked periods</h4><p>Once a month is locked (Imports &amp; Feeds, Periods Built), nothing that would change it is accepted: the revenue run, a write-off, a rate reset, reversing a restructure and reopening an EIR are all refused with the message "Reporting period ... is locked: ... cannot be written."</p>'
                . '<h4>What happens next</h4><p>A locked EIR feeds the monthly revenue run and the discount rate of the loan\'s expected credit loss. Reopening it therefore invalidates both, which is why it needs a reason and an administrator.</p>',
            'steps' => [
                'In the sidebar, under Financial modelling, open EIR & Revenue Recognition.',
                'Check the data on the EIR Data tabs, starting with Contract Master.',
                'Approve the accounting rules on Accounting Rules, and classify and review the fees on Fee Classification.',
                'Generate and approve the version 1 schedules on EIR Data, Schedule Review.',
                'Calculate the EIRs on EIR Calculations, Calculations, and have a second person approve and lock them.',
                'Run the month-end and read GL Reconciliation and EIR as at a Date.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-data' => 'The EIR Data entry with its row of tabs, open on Contract Master'],
        ],

        'EIR data' => [
            'body' => '<p>The EIR Data entry holds the records the EIR is built from. This article covers its first two tabs, <b>Contract Master</b> (one row per contract with its terms) and <b>Cash Flows</b> (every contractual cash flow by due date), and the <b>GL Postings</b> view of the same screen. Finance officers and reviewers use them to check what was loaded before anything is solved. In the sidebar, under <b>Financial modelling</b>, open <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b>, choose <b>EIR Data</b> and then <b>Contract Master</b> or <b>Cash Flows</b> (breadcrumb: Financial modelling &gt; EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition &gt; EIR Data &gt; Contract Master). The screen needs the <b>eir.view</b> permission. The Schedule Review tab of the same screen has its own article, Schedule review and approval.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>The tab row of EIR Data, then the page title, which is the name of the tab (<b>Contract Master</b>, <b>Cash Flows</b>), with a one-line description: "Facility terms, source conventions, fee and schedule coverage for every contract" or "Contractual principal, interest and fee cash flows by due date".</li>'
                . '<li>Four tiles: <b>Contracts</b>, <b>Cash-flow rows</b>, <b>GL postings</b> and <b>Locked EIRs</b>, each a count over the whole book.</li>'
                . '<li>A panel headed <b>Contracts</b> or <b>Contractual cash flows</b>, with "n record(s)" beneath (and "matching the search" when a search is set).</li>'
                . '<li>A search box, "Contract, account or reference", and the <b>Search</b> button.</li>'
                . '<li>Under the table, "Showing a to b of n" and the page links <b>Previous</b>, the page numbers and <b>Next</b>, fifteen rows a page.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>The Contract Master table</h4><ul>'
                . '<li><b>Contract</b>: the contract number, with the customer name beneath (from the contract master, or from the latest loan book when the master has none; "Customer name unavailable" when neither does).</li>'
                . '<li><b>Portfolio / product</b>: the portfolio, with the product type beneath.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Terms</b>: origination to maturity date ("Dates not recorded" when both are missing); the frequency (<b>Annual</b>, <b>Semi-annual</b>, <b>Quarterly</b>, <b>Bi-monthly</b>, <b>Monthly</b> or "Frequency pending"); the contractual rate in a purple chip; and, for a loan priced over the prime lending rate, a blue chip <b>PLR + x.xx</b> with the derived spread. The chip turns red and adds "drifts" when the spread moved across the loan books by more than the tolerance: the loan is held for review (hover over the chip for the explanation).</li>'
                . '<li><b>Tenor / repayment</b>: the tenor in months ("Tenor pending"), a chip with the repayment type worked out from the schedule (<b>Capital + interest</b>, <b>Interest only</b>, <b>Capital only</b> or <b>Repayment type pending</b>) and "Moratorium: n months" or "None".</li>'
                . '<li><b>Amounts</b>: the drawn amount and currency, with "Approved" and the approved amount beneath.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Source coverage</b>: the number of cash flows and fees held for the contract, with the source system of its terms beneath ("Source not recorded").</li>'
                . '<li><b>EIR status</b>: a badge, <b>PENDING</b> (amber), <b>CALCULATED</b> (blue), <b>BLOCKED</b> (red), <b>REOPENED</b> or <b>LOCKED</b> (green), with the effective annual rate beneath once solved.</li></ul>'
                . '<p>The search on Contract Master matches the contract number, customer name, sub-account, portfolio and product type.</p>'
                . '<h4>The Cash Flows table</h4><p>Columns <b>Contract</b> (with the customer beneath), <b>Version</b> (v1 is the original schedule; v2 and later are loaded by an approved restructure), <b>Due date</b>, <b>Principal</b>, <b>Interest</b>, <b>Fees</b>, <b>Total due</b> and <b>Source</b> (GENERATED, TAKEON_WORKBOOK or the source system, with the source reference beneath). Rows are in contract and due-date order. The search matches the contract number and the source reference.</p>'
                . '<h4>The GL Postings view</h4><p>The <b>GL Postings</b> button on GL Reconciliation opens this screen on the interest postings (the screen title reads <b>GL Reconciliation</b>, list <b>GL interest postings</b>). Six cards: <b>Calculated rows</b>, <b>Within tolerance</b>, <b>Exceptions</b>, <b>Matched GL interest</b>, <b>Calculated EIR interest</b> and <b>Net EIR minus GL</b>, with the note that variance is calculated EIR interest minus GL interest and that the tolerance is the band approved in the Governance Centre for each month. Columns <b>Contract</b>, <b>Period</b>, <b>GL account</b>, <b>GL interest</b>, <b>EIR interest</b>, <b>EIR minus GL</b> (amber when positive, red when negative), <b>Variance %</b>, <b>Status</b> (<b>Within tolerance</b>, <b>Variance</b>, <b>No approved band</b> or <b>Not calculated</b>) and <b>Source</b>. An amber line counts postings that fall in a month with no approved band: they are counted but not judged.</p>'
                . '<h4>Buttons and icons</h4><ul><li><b>EIR Data Intake</b> (above the table, at its top right): opens the import screen. It needs the <b>settings</b> permission (or <b>eir.fees.import</b> for fees only).</li><li><b>Calculate EIR</b> (above the table, at its top right): opens EIR Calculations, Calculations; it needs <b>settings</b>.</li><li><b>Search</b>: applies the search box.</li></ul><p>The Contract Master and Cash Flows tabs only read; nothing on them changes a record.</p>'
                . '<h4>To check a contract before it is solved</h4><ol><li>Open Contract Master and type the contract number in the search box, then press <b>Search</b>.</li><li>Read the Terms column: dates, frequency, rate and spread chip.</li><li>Read Source coverage: a contract with no cash flows has no schedule yet; generate it on Schedule Review.</li><li>Open Cash Flows and search the same contract to read its schedule row by row.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li>"No records found. Use EIR Data Intake to load data." on Contract Master and Cash Flows when nothing is loaded or nothing matches.</li><li>"No GL postings match this search." on the GL Postings view.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>"Frequency pending" or "Dates not recorded".</b> The contract master did not state the term. Correct it at source and load Extract A again; the contract stays blocked until then.</li><li><b>A red "drifts" chip.</b> The spread over the prime lending rate is not constant across the loan books; see Reference rates and the spread over prime.</li><li><b>The EIR status reads PENDING for every contract.</b> No calculation has been run yet; see EIR calculations, approval and reopening.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR & Revenue Recognition, EIR Data, Contract Master.',
                'Type a contract number, customer, portfolio or product in the search box and press Search.',
                'Read the Terms, Tenor / repayment, Amounts and Source coverage columns.',
                'Check the EIR status badge and, once solved, the effective annual rate beneath it.',
                'Open the Cash Flows tab and search the same contract to read its schedule by due date.',
            ],
            'images' => [
                'eir-data' => 'EIR Data, Contract Master: the four tiles and the contract table',
                'eir-data-cashflows' => 'EIR Data, Cash Flows: the contractual cash flows by due date',
            ],
            'routes' => ['eir-data.index'],
        ],

        'Drawdowns and undrawn commitments' => [
            'body' => '<p>Drawdowns records the money actually paid out on each facility, tranche by tranche, and reports the part of a facility approved but not yet drawn. In the sidebar, under <b>Financial modelling</b>, open <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b>, choose <b>EIR Data</b> and then the <b>Drawdowns</b> tab (breadcrumb: Financial modelling &gt; EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition &gt; EIR Data &gt; Drawdowns). It needs the <b>eir.view</b> permission and only reads; loading a file goes through EIR Data Intake.</p>'
                . '<h4>Why the screen exists</h4><p>A facility is often paid out in stages. Three things depend on knowing the date and the amount of each stage. Interest in the month of a payment can only be rebuilt from the day the money left, so a month with a new tranche in it cannot be reconciled to the ledger without it. A later payment is money going out again after the loan started, and the EIR has to see it as such. And the part still not drawn is a commitment MAIIC has made, which IFRS 9 asks to be reported separately from the loan itself.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>Title <b>Drawdowns</b>, strapline "Every tranche paid out on a facility, and the commitment still undrawn".</li>'
                . '<li>Five tiles: <b>Approved</b> (with the number of facilities), <b>Drawn</b> ("up to" the date chosen), <b>Undrawn commitment</b> ("reported separately under IFRS 9"), <b>Drawdowns loaded</b> (tranche rows, with the number of facilities that have their own rows) and <b>Without drawdown rows</b> (amber when above nil: how many come from the loan book and how many are not known).</li>'
                . '<li>The panel <b>Facilities, largest undrawn commitment first</b> with the line "Choose a facility to see each tranche. Source says where the amount drawn came from." and a <b>How this works</b> link that opens the explanation.</li>'
                . '<li>The filter: <b>Drawn up to</b> (a date; only drawdowns on or before it are counted, today by default), a <b>Facility number</b> box, <b>Apply</b>, and <b>Clear</b> once a filter differs from the default.</li>'
                . '<li>The table: <b>Facility</b> (the account number as a link), <b>Customer</b>, <b>Approved</b>, <b>Drawn</b>, <b>Undrawn commitment</b>, <b>Tranches</b> and <b>Source</b>, with a total row "Total, n facilities" at the foot and the page links under it.</li>'
                . '<li>Choosing a facility number opens its tranches beneath: <b>Tranche</b>, <b>Date drawn</b>, <b>Amount</b>, <b>Of which capitalised fees</b> (fee debits on the loan account the same day, included in the amount), <b>Reference</b>, <b>Sub-account</b> and <b>Loaded</b>, with a <b>Total drawn</b> line; fifteen tranches a page. Choosing it again closes them.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>How each figure is worked out</h4><ul><li><b>Approved</b> is the sanctioned amount from the contract master, or from the loan book where the master has none.</li><li><b>Drawn</b> is the sum of the tranches up to the <b>Drawn up to</b> date, so it can be struck at any date.</li><li><b>Undrawn commitment</b> is approved less drawn, never below nil.</li><li><b>Source</b>: <b>Drawdown rows loaded</b> (green, the good case); <b>Loan book, disbursed column</b> (amber, with the month beside it: no drawdown rows are loaded, so the figure carries no dates); <b>Contract master</b>; or <b>Not known</b>.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Not known is not nil</h4><p>Where no source states the approved amount or the amount drawn, the row reads <b>not known</b> and adds nothing to the total. An empty table is not evidence that a facility has been drawn in full.</p>'
                . '<h4>Buttons and icons</h4><ul><li><b>Download sample CSV</b> (below the tiles, above the facilities table at its top right): the drawdown import\'s columns with worked example rows.</li><li><b>Import drawdowns</b> (beside it): opens EIR Data Intake already set to <b>Drawdowns, one row per tranche paid out</b>. The intake needs the <b>settings</b> permission; a user with only eir.view is refused there.</li><li>A facility number: opens or closes its tranches.</li><li><b>Apply</b> and <b>Clear</b>: apply or reset the filter.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Loading the file</h4><p>One row per payment with three required columns: <code>contract_id</code> (the loan account number), <code>disbursement_date</code> and <code>amount</code>; optional <code>sub_account_no</code>, <code>tranche_no</code> and <code>reference</code>. Dates must be written year first (2026-09-24): any other shape refuses the whole file and names the column and the row. Every amount must be above zero; a reversal is corrected in the core system. The same file loaded twice adds nothing; two payments of the same amount on the same day with no reference are read as one row sent twice, so give each its own reference.</p>'
                . '<h4>The register built from the ledger</h4><p>The bootstrap also builds the register from E-Banker\'s ledger: one row per drawdown date, from the type-301 postings. A fee capitalised into the loan is added to the drawdown of its date (the <b>Of which capitalised fees</b> column), never a tranche of its own. The opening balance posted at the take-on of 31 July 2024 is the migration, not money lent that day. A rebuild replaces only the rows an earlier build wrote (references starting CUMVOUCH:); rows loaded from a file are left alone.</p>'
                . '<h4>What happens next</h4><p>The dated tranches feed the interest reconciliation (a mid-month tranche), the EIR (each later payment out is a cash flow on its date) and the schedule of a facility drawn in tranches. Every load is written to the audit log.</p>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li>"No facilities to show. Import the drawdown file or load the contract master first."</li><li>Under a facility with no rows: "No drawdown rows are loaded for this facility. Import the drawdown file to date each tranche; until then the amount drawn comes from the loan book."</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>"The file was not loaded: disbursement_date on row 7 is \'15/09/2026\'."</b> Save the file with dates written year first and load it again. Nothing was loaded.</li><li><b>A facility shows the loan book as its source.</b> Its drawdown rows have not been loaded; no month inside it can be reconciled.</li><li><b>The undrawn commitment looks too big.</b> Check <b>Drawn up to</b>: a tranche paid after it is not counted.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR & Revenue Recognition, EIR Data, Drawdowns, and read the five tiles.',
                'Set Drawn up to to the month end you are reporting and press Apply.',
                'Choose a facility number to see each tranche, its date, reference and any capitalised fee.',
                'To load a new file, press Import drawdowns; the intake opens on the drawdowns type.',
                'Make sure every date is written year first and every amount is above zero, then import the file.',
                'Give any facility whose source reads Loan book, disbursed column to Finance as an outstanding data request.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-drawdowns' => 'Drawdowns: approved, drawn and undrawn per facility, with one facility\'s tranches open'],
            'routes' => ['eir-drawdowns.index'],
        ],

        'Reference rates and the spread over prime' => [
            'body' => '<p>Reference Rates shows the Reserve Bank prime lending rate (PLR) as a dated series: one row for every date the rate was recorded, with the rate that applied from that day. MAIIC\'s Industrial and Agricultural loans are priced as the PLR plus a fixed spread, so when the PLR moves those loans reprice (IFRS 9 B5.4.5). FinES loans are fixed at 10 percent and do not. In the sidebar, under <b>Financial modelling</b>, open <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b>, choose <b>EIR Data</b> and then the <b>Reference Rates</b> tab (breadcrumb: Financial modelling &gt; EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition &gt; EIR Data &gt; Reference Rates). It needs the <b>eir.view</b> permission and only reads.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>Title <b>Reference Rates</b>, strapline "The PLR series by effective date: the rate every PLR-linked loan reprices from".</li>'
                . '<li>Six tiles: <b>PLR rate in force</b> ("since" the last date), <b>Rate changes</b> ("including the opening rate"), <b>Rows loaded</b> (and how many repeat the previous rate), <b>First date</b>, <b>Last date</b> and <b>Repaired rows</b> ("day and month swapped back", amber when above nil).</li>'
                . '<li>The panel <b>PLR series, newest first</b> with the note that Change is the move from the previous row in percentage points and a grey row left the rate where it was, and a <b>How this works</b> link explaining the spread derivation and the date rule.</li>'
                . '<li>The table: <b>Effective date</b>, <b>Rate</b>, <b>Change</b> ("opening rate" on the first row, "no change" on a repeat, otherwise for example +1.50 pts in red for a rise or green for a fall), <b>Source row</b>, <b>As delivered</b> (what the cell held in the file) and <b>How it was read</b> (amber when the row was repaired). Fifteen rows a page with <b>Previous</b> and <b>Next</b>.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Buttons and icons</h4><ul><li><b>Index</b> selector (in the series panel, beside its title): shown only when more than one series is loaded. Today there is one, PLR.</li><li><b>Download sample CSV</b> (above the table, at its top right): the reference-rate import\'s columns with worked example rows.</li><li><b>Import reference rates</b> (beside it): opens EIR Data Intake already set to <b>Reference rates (File C), the prime lending rate by effective date</b>. The intake needs the <b>settings</b> permission.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Why the dates must be written year first</h4><p>E-Banker wrote dates such as 03/04/2020: 3 April in Malawi, 4 March in a spreadsheet set to American dates, and Excel silently chose the second reading for 20 of 48 rows. A reference rate on the wrong date puts the wrong rate in force for weeks. The importer therefore accepts only dates written year first (2020-04-03) and refuses the whole file, naming the column and the first bad row, if any date is written another way. Dates must rise strictly down the file. A row that repeats the previous rate is loaded and counted but is not a rate change. A date already stored with a different rate is never overwritten: the row is listed as skipped so a person can decide.</p>'
                . '<h4>How the spread over prime is worked out and checked</h4><p>E-Banker does not export the spread as a field, so it is derived: for every month a loan appears in the loan book, the loan-book rate less the PLR in force at that month end. If the result is the same every month (within 0.15 percentage points) the median is stored as the derived spread and shown on Contract Master as <b>PLR + x.xx</b>. A loan whose monthly spread wanders by more is not given a spread and is flagged (the red "drifts" chip), because a wrong spread would misprice every reset. Where MAIIC supplied its own spread, it is compared with the derived one and both are kept side by side.</p>'
                . '<h4>To load a new rate file</h4><ol><li>Save the E-Banker rate table as a CSV with the dates written year first.</li><li>Press <b>Import reference rates</b>; the intake opens on the reference-rate type.</li><li>Choose the file. <code>effective_date</code> and <code>rate</code> are required; <code>index_code</code>, <code>source_row</code>, <code>as_delivered</code> and <code>interpretation</code> are optional.</li><li>Press <b>Import file</b>. The result shows <b>Rates loaded</b>, <b>Already stored</b>, <b>Rate changes in the file</b>, <b>Rows repeating the previous rate</b> and the rate now in force, with an <b>Open Reference Rates</b> link.</li><li>Back on Reference Rates, check the rate in force and the last date against the source.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>From the command line</h4><p><code>php artisan eir:import-reference-rates path/to/file.csv --user=ID</code> loads a file with the same checks (<code>--index=PLR</code> names the series when the file has no index column). <code>php artisan eir:derive-spreads</code> derives the spread for every contract and lists those that drift; <code>--period=YYYY-MM</code> reads loan-book months up to that month.</p>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li>"No PLR rates are loaded yet. Import the reference-rate file to start the series."</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>"effective_date on row N is \'03/04/2020\', which is not a date written year first."</b> Re-save the file with ISO dates, keeping the column as text.</li><li><b>"effective_date on row N is earlier than row M."</b> Sort by date and check that row.</li><li><b>A rate is listed as skipped with "stored rate differs from the file\'s".</b> Confirm which is right with Credit before anything is changed.</li><li><b>Every loan is held for a drifting spread.</b> The series is probably missing a rate change; check the last date against the Reserve Bank\'s announcements.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR & Revenue Recognition, EIR Data, Reference Rates, and read the rate in force, the last date and the rate changes.',
                'To load a new file, press Import reference rates; the intake opens on the reference-rate type.',
                'Make sure every date in the file is written year first and the rows rise by date.',
                'Choose the file and press Import file; read the result tiles or the refusal, which names the column and the row.',
                'Back on Reference Rates, confirm the new rows and the change from the previous rate.',
                'After the month\'s loan book is in, run php artisan eir:derive-spreads and review any contract listed as drifting.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-reference-rates' => 'Reference Rates: the PLR series newest first, with the change from the previous rate'],
            'routes' => ['eir-reference-rates.index'],
        ],

        'Trial balances' => [
            'body' => '<p>Trial Balances holds Finance\'s monthly E-Banker trial balance, which the GL reconciliation, the fee GL check and the bridge to the annual financial statements read. Each file is loaded through the landing zone\'s gates, so an auditor can see the exact file every GL figure came from. In the sidebar, under <b>Financial modelling</b>, open <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b>, choose <b>EIR Data</b> and then the <b>Trial Balances</b> tab (breadcrumb: Financial modelling &gt; EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition &gt; EIR Data &gt; Trial Balances). Anyone with <b>eir.view</b> can read it; uploading needs <b>eir.govern</b>.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>Title <b>Trial Balances</b>, strapline "Finance\'s monthly trial balance from E-Banker: loaded through the landing zone\'s gates, read by the GL reconciliation and the AFS bridge".</li>'
                . '<li>Four tiles: <b>Months held</b> (post-closing months), <b>First month</b>, <b>Latest month</b> and <b>Refused files</b> (red when above nil).</li>'
                . '<li>The panel <b>Months held</b>: "One row per month and basis. Post-closing is the month-end balance; pre-closing is December before the year-end closing entries (from the AFS bridge)." Columns <b>Month</b>, <b>Basis</b> (a green <b>Post-closing</b> or gold <b>Pre-closing</b> badge), <b>GL lines</b>, <b>Debits</b>, <b>Credits</b>, <b>Difference</b> (red when it is not nil) and <b>Source file</b>, newest first.</li>'
                . '<li>The panel <b>Loads</b>: "Every file loaded, with its gates. A refused file keeps its rows in the landing zone but nothing reads them." Columns <b>Load</b> (#number), <b>File</b>, <b>Month</b>, <b>Lines</b>, <b>Status</b> (green <b>Landed</b> or red <b>Refused</b>), <b>Gates</b> (one line per gate: <b>Period known</b>, <b>Rows found</b>, <b>Numeric</b> and <b>Debits equal credits and grand total ties</b>, each "passed" or "failed" in red, with its detail; hover over a failed gate for the failures) and <b>Loaded</b> (date and time, and who loaded it, or System). The latest 50 loads are listed.</li>'
                . '<li>Both tables page fifteen rows at a time with <b>Previous</b> and <b>Next</b>.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Buttons and icons</h4><ul><li><b>Upload trial balance</b> (upload arrow, above the tables): opens the upload window. Shown only to a user with <b>eir.govern</b>.</li><li>In the window <b>Upload a trial balance</b>: the <b>How to import</b> panel with <b>Check file</b> (reads the file through every gate and lists each problem; nothing is saved), <b>Download sample file</b> (a layout with two illustrative lines that tie) and <b>Download column guide</b> (the same as an Excel workbook), and a link to show or hide the column table; <b>File</b> (.xls or .xlsx, up to 50 MB); <b>Basis</b> (<code>Post-closing (month-end)</code> or <code>Pre-closing (December, before closing)</code>); <b>Month (only if the file has no date)</b>; <b>Sheet (optional)</b> ("the first sheet" when blank); <b>Cancel</b>; and <b>Load trial balance</b> (greyed out until a file is chosen; reads "Loading..." while it works).</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>How a file is read and checked</h4><p>The trial balance is read by position: the period from the report itself (cell B1), GL title in column B, Debit in C, Credit in D, and the Grand Total row. Before anything is kept the gates run: the month must be known, GL rows must be found, every Debit and Credit cell must be a number, debits must equal credits and the lines must add up to the report\'s grand total. A file that fails a gate is refused and listed under Loads with the gate that failed; its rows are kept in the landing zone for the audit trail but nothing reads them.</p>'
                . '<h4>To load a month</h4><ol><li>Export the month\'s Trial Balance report from E-Banker as xls or xlsx, one month per file.</li><li>Press <b>Upload trial balance</b> and choose the file.</li><li>Leave <b>Basis</b> on Post-closing for a month end; choose Pre-closing only for December before the closing entries.</li><li>Give the month only if the file carries no date. Press <b>Check file</b> to see any problem first.</li><li>Press <b>Load trial balance</b> and read the message.</li><li>Check the month under <b>Months held</b>: the difference should be nil.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>What happens next</h4><p>A landed file is kept with its SHA-256 fingerprint; its GL lines are then written to the GL side read by the reconciliation and the fee GL check. Within a month and basis the zone holds, the GL lines become exactly the landed file: a corrected file for a month already held replaces that month\'s lines, and the earlier version stays in the landing zone. A month the landing zone does not hold at all (loaded before the landing zone existed, for example by the older import) is left as it is, so uploading one month never changes or empties any other month. Loading the same file twice changes nothing. Each load is written to the audit log.</p>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li>"No trial balance is held yet. Use Upload trial balance above the table." and "No trial balance has been loaded from a file yet."</li><li>"Trial balance for 2026-09 loaded (load 12): n GL lines, debits ..., credits ...; m GL lines now held." (illustrative figures)</li><li>"This file was already loaded (load 12); nothing changed."</li><li>"Trial balance refused (load 13): ..." with the failures, and "The trial balance could not be read: ..." when the file cannot be opened.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>Period known failed.</b> The report has no period stamp: give the month in the window and load again.</li><li><b>Debits equal credits and grand total ties failed.</b> The export was cut short or edited; export it again from E-Banker.</li><li><b>No Upload trial balance button.</b> You do not hold eir.govern.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Export the month\'s Trial Balance report from E-Banker as xls or xlsx, one month per file.',
                'Open EIR & Revenue Recognition, EIR Data, Trial Balances, and press Upload trial balance.',
                'Choose the file; leave Basis on Post-closing unless it is December before the closing entries.',
                'Give the month only if the file carries no date, and press Check file to see any problem first.',
                'Press Load trial balance and read the message: the month, the GL lines and the debit and credit totals.',
                'Check the month under Months held (the difference should be nil) and any refused file under Loads.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-trial-balances' => 'Trial Balances: the months held and every load with its gates'],
            'routes' => ['eir-trial-balances.index', 'eir-trial-balances.upload'],
        ],

        'Write-offs' => [
            'body' => '<p>Write-offs shows every write-off the system knows of: those the E-Banker ledger carries, and a register for write-offs that are not on the loan account. A write-off is not a collection: the EIR revenue treats it as derecognition, never as cash received. In the sidebar, under <b>Financial modelling</b>, open <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b>, choose <b>EIR Data</b> and then the <b>Write-offs</b> tab (breadcrumb: Financial modelling &gt; EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition &gt; EIR Data &gt; Write-offs). Anyone with <b>eir.view</b> can read it; proposing, approving, rejecting, withdrawing and reversing need <b>eir.govern</b>.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>Title <b>Write-offs</b>, strapline "Every write-off the system knows of: those the E-Banker ledger carries, and the register for write-offs that are not on the loan account".</li>'
                . '<li>Three tiles: <b>In the ledger</b> (count, with the amount beneath), <b>Approved in the register</b> (count and amount) and <b>Awaiting approval</b> (amber when above nil).</li>'
                . '<li>The panel <b>Write-offs in the ledger</b>: a credit to a loan account is a write-off when its type is 300, when its batch debits GL 1064 (Term Loans Write Offs), or when it is an ACCOUNT CLOSURE in a TRF batch on an account whose status is H (written off). Columns <b>Date</b>, <b>Loan</b> (customer, with the contract beneath), <b>Amount</b>, <b>Batch</b> (with "type" and the transaction type beneath), <b>Narration</b> and <b>Why it is a write-off</b> (<code>type 300</code>, <code>batch ... debits GL 1064</code> or <code>ACCOUNT CLOSURE in a TRF batch on a status-H account</code>). Newest first, fifteen a page. These need no approval: E-Banker\'s posting is the evidence.</li>'
                . '<li>The panel <b>Write-off register</b>: for a write-off that is not on the loan account, for example one made only in the audit adjustments or at GL level. Columns <b>#</b>, <b>Loan</b>, <b>Date</b>, <b>Amount</b>, <b>Reason and evidence</b> (with the GL reference), <b>Status</b> (a badge <b>Proposed</b> (gold), <b>Approved</b> (green), <b>Rejected</b> (red), <b>Withdrawn</b> or <b>Reversed</b> (grey), with "Proposed by", "Approved by" and who closed it with the reason) and <b>Actions</b>. Fifteen a page.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Buttons and icons</h4><ul>'
                . '<li><b>Propose write-off</b> (plus sign, above the Write-off register at its top right): opens <b>Propose a write-off</b>. Needs <b>eir.govern</b>.</li>'
                . '<li>Tick (<b>Approve</b>): on a Proposed entry, shown only to an eir.govern user who did not propose it. A confirmation "Approve write-off #n?" says it is written to the loan\'s actual transactions as a write-off; press <b>Approve</b>.</li>'
                . '<li>Cross (<b>Reject</b>): on a Proposed entry, shown to an eir.govern user who did not propose it. You are asked for the reason.</li>'
                . '<li>No-entry circle (<b>Withdraw</b>): on a Proposed entry, shown only to the person who proposed it. Asks for the reason.</li>'
                . '<li>Clock with an arrow (<b>Reverse</b>): on an Approved entry. Asks for the reason; the write-off row is removed from the loan\'s transactions. Refused when the month of the write-off is locked.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>The propose window</h4><p>"Only for a write-off that is not posted on the loan account in E-Banker. A write-off E-Banker posted (type 300, or a batch to GL 1064) is already counted." Fields: <b>Loan</b> (<code>Choose the loan</code>, then every loan in the loan book by customer and contract), <b>Write-off date</b>, <b>Amount written off</b> (more than nil), <b>Reason</b> ("Why the loan is written off", up to 500 characters), <b>Evidence</b> ("Where it is recorded: board minute, audit adjustment, GL batch", required) and <b>GL reference (optional)</b>. Buttons <b>Cancel</b> and <b>Propose</b>.</p>'
                . '<h4>To record a write-off that is not on the loan account</h4><ol><li>Check that it is not already listed under <b>Write-offs in the ledger</b>; if it is, nothing more is needed.</li><li>Press <b>Propose write-off</b>, choose the loan, give the date, amount, reason and evidence, and press <b>Propose</b>.</li><li>A different person with eir.govern opens the screen and presses the tick (or the cross with a reason).</li><li>After approval, re-run the EIR revenue from the month of the write-off so the amortised cost reflects it.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>What happens next</h4><p>Each proposal is a row in the loan_write_offs register. On approval the write-off is written to the loan\'s actual transactions as a Write-off (source WRITE_OFF_REGISTER), which the revenue run reads as derecognition. A rejected or withdrawn entry never reaches the engines. Every step is in the audit trail ("Write-off Proposed", "Write-off Approved", "Write-off Rejected" and so on).</p>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li>"No write-off in the ledger." and "No write-off in the register. Use Propose write-off above this table, at its top right, for one that is not on the loan account."</li><li>"Write-off #n proposed. A different person approves it before it reaches the engines."</li><li>"Write-off #n approved: it is on the loan\'s actual transactions as a write-off; re-run the EIR revenue from its month."</li><li>"Write-off #n: rejected." (or withdrawn, reversed).</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>"The person who proposed a write-off cannot approve it."</b> Ask a colleague with eir.govern.</li><li><b>"Contract n already has a write-off proposed or approved in the register."</b> Only one open entry per loan.</li><li><b>"Contract n is not in the loan book."</b> Check the contract number.</li><li><b>"Reporting period ... is locked: a write-off cannot be written."</b> The month is closed; unlock it with a reason on Imports &amp; Feeds, Periods Built, only if the restatement is deliberate.</li><li><b>"Only the person who proposed a write-off can withdraw it, and only before it is approved."</b> and <b>"Only an approved write-off can be reversed."</b></li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR & Revenue Recognition, EIR Data, Write-offs.',
                'Check whether the write-off is already listed under Write-offs in the ledger; if it is, nothing more is needed.',
                'If not, press Propose write-off, choose the loan, give the date, amount, reason and evidence, and press Propose.',
                'Ask a colleague with eir.govern to press the tick to approve it, or the cross to reject it with a reason.',
                'After approval, re-run the EIR revenue from the month of the write-off.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-write-offs' => 'Write-offs: the ledger write-offs and the governed register'],
            'routes' => ['eir-write-offs.index', 'eir-write-offs.store', 'eir-write-offs.approve', 'eir-write-offs.close'],
        ],

        'Restructures and rate resets' => [
            'body' => '<p>Restructures &amp; Resets is where a change to a loan\'s terms is found, decided and booked, and where every move of a floating loan\'s rate is recorded. In the sidebar, under <b>Financial modelling</b>, open <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b>, then <b>Restructures &amp; Resets</b> (breadcrumb: Financial modelling &gt; EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition &gt; Restructures &amp; Resets). Anyone with <b>eir.view</b> can read it; finding candidates, classifying, approving, rejecting, withdrawing and reversing need <b>eir.govern</b>.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>Title <b>Restructures &amp; Resets</b> and its strapline.</li>'
                . '<li>Five tiles: <b>Candidates</b> ("n cannot be booked"), <b>Awaiting approval</b>, <b>Confirmed</b> ("n restructured"), <b>Booked</b> ("n await the run, m blocked", red when any is blocked) and <b>Rate moves</b> ("n spread changes, m approved").</li>'
                . '<li>An amber notice, with an X to close it: "A candidate is evidence that a loan\'s terms may have changed, never a confirmed restructure. Nothing here reaches the accounts until one person classifies it with a reason and a different person approves. The engine\'s suggestion is only a suggestion."</li>'
                . '<li>Three page tabs: <b>Restructure register</b> (with its count), <b>IFRS 9 note</b> (the modifications the note discloses for the year) and <b>Rate resets</b> (with its count). The open tab is kept in the address.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>The Restructure register tab</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>Filters (applied as you change them): <b>Status</b> (<code>All</code>, <code>Candidates</code>, <code>Proposed</code>, <code>Confirmed</code>), <b>Engine\'s suggestion</b>, <b>Evidence</b> (<code>Chart regenerated</code>, <code>Dated copies of the chart</code>, <code>MODIFICATION_LOG edit</code>, <code>Rate change</code>, <code>Migration worksheet (CV_10)</code>), <b>Loan</b> ("Name or contract") and the tick box <b>Only those that can be booked</b>.</li>'
                . '<li>Columns: <b>#</b>, <b>Loan</b>, <b>Date</b> ("No date" when unknown), <b>Evidence</b> (source and reference), <b>What changed</b> (maturity, instalments due, instalment, balance, any amount capitalised in amber, rate: each "old to new"), <b>Engine\'s suggestion</b> (badge <b>Modification</b>, <b>Uncertain</b>, <b>Revised estimate</b>, <b>Contractual reset</b> or <b>Correction</b>, the reason, and in red "Cannot be booked: ..." when the candidate has no effective date, no old terms, or a new schedule that amortises more than the borrower owes), <b>Status</b> (<b>Candidate</b>, <b>Proposed</b> or <b>Confirmed</b>, with the classification, reason, Board approval, qualitative factors and who proposed and approved; "No Board approval recorded (directive 16(1))" in amber on a modification without one), <b>Accounting</b> and <b>Actions</b>.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Accounting</b> shows what the revenue run did: <b>Awaits the revenue run</b>, <b>Booked</b> (the treatment <b>Modified (5.4.3)</b>, <b>Derecognised; new loan recognised</b> or <b>Catch-up (B5.4.6)</b>, the carrying amount before and after, the change against the threshold, the gain or loss and the EIR from now), <b>Blocked</b> with the reason, or <b>Reversed</b>; for a confirmed event with no accounting, "No accounting:" and why.</li>'
                . '<li>On the <b>IFRS 9 note</b> tab, the panel <b>Modifications in the IFRS 9 note, (year) (IFRS 7.35J and 7.20A)</b> with the lines for lifetime-ECL modifications, all modifications not derecognised, derecognitions, the B5.4.6 catch-up and modified loans now on 12-month ECL.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>The Rate resets tab</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>Filters: <b>Classification</b> (<code>All</code>, <code>Awaiting a classification</code>, <code>Proposed</code>, <code>Approved</code>), <b>Type</b> (<code>Moved with the PLR</code>, <code>Spread changed</code>) and <b>Loan</b>.</li>'
                . '<li>Each prime-rate reset of a floating loan, found once from the PLR series on the spread locked at drawdown and set against the rate the loan book shows E-Banker charged, and each change of the spread as its own row (a PLR reset and a spread change in the same month are two rows on one date, each classified on its own). A move with the PLR that the reset pipeline cannot take (the loan is held there, or the engine treats it as fixed-rate) is still listed, marked held, with the reason. Columns: a tick box (not on approved rows; the header box selects the page), <b>Loan</b>, <b>Month</b> (with "from" the reset date), <b>Type</b> (green <b>Moved with the PLR</b> or gold <b>Spread changed</b>), <b>Rate</b>, <b>PLR</b> and <b>Spread</b> (each "old to new"), <b>Evidence</b>, <b>Status</b> (<b>Awaiting a classification</b>, <b>Proposed</b>, <b>Approved</b>, with the classification <b>In-contract reset</b> or <b>Negotiated</b>, "Suggested: in-contract", the reason, who proposed and approved, and "Restructure candidate #n" for a negotiated move; under it "Reset pipeline:" detected, Reset schedule built (with the schedule version), held (with the reason) or, for a spread change, not scheduled) and <b>Actions</b>. Neither the classification nor the pipeline moves the EIR: a floating loan\'s EIR follows the rate charged each month (decision D38).</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Buttons and icons</h4><ul>'
                . '<li><b>Find candidates</b> (magnifier, top right, eir.govern): asks "Find candidates?" and then reads the reschedule history (RS_02a to RS_02f, the live chart), the migration worksheet, the loan-book rates and the PLR series again. Candidates no one has acted on are refreshed; nothing is confirmed. The button reads "Reading the history..." while it works, which can take a minute.</li>'
                . '<li>Pencil (<b>Classify</b>): on a Candidate; opens <b>Classify event #n</b>.</li>'
                . '<li>Tick (<b>Approve</b>): on a Proposed event, shown only to someone other than the proposer. The confirmation says whether it changes the accounting.</li>'
                . '<li>Cross (<b>Reject</b>), no-entry circle (<b>Withdraw</b>, proposer only) and clock with an arrow (<b>Reverse</b>, on a Confirmed event): each asks for a reason and returns the event to a candidate. Reversing removes the booked restructure and its schedule version while its month is open; a later confirmed restructure of the same loan must be reversed first.</li>'
                . '<li>On Rate resets: <b>Propose as in-contract (n)</b> and <b>Propose as negotiated (n)</b> (for the ticked moves awaiting a classification; each asks for a reason), <b>Approve (n)</b> (for ticked proposed moves you did not propose), and on a proposed row the cross (<b>Reject</b>) or, for the proposer, the no-entry circle (<b>Withdraw</b>).</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>The classification window</h4><p>The window repeats the engine\'s suggestion and any "Cannot be booked" reason ("It can still be classified as a correction or as not a change."). Fields: <b>Classification</b> (<code>Modification</code>, <code>Revised estimate</code>, <code>Contractual reset</code>, <code>Correction</code>, <code>Not a change</code>), with a line explaining the one chosen; <b>Reason</b> (required). For a modification also: <b>Board approval (directive 16(1))</b> and <b>Date of the approval</b>; the qualitative factors (any one derecognises the loan whatever the threshold test says): a different borrower, a change of currency, a feature that fails the SPPI test, a change of instrument; <b>Fair value of the new loan (only if derecognised)</b> with its <b>Basis of the fair value</b> (required with a fair value); and <b>Fees received (+) or costs incurred (-) on the modification</b>. Buttons <b>Cancel</b> and <b>Propose</b> (greyed out until a classification is chosen).</p>'
                . '<h4>What each classification books</h4><ul><li><b>Modification</b> (IFRS 9 5.4.3): on approval E-Banker\'s new schedule is loaded as the next schedule version; the revenue run of the event\'s month tests it against <b>The derecognition test</b> in the Governance Centre (10 percent by default). Below it, the gross carrying amount becomes the present value of the new flows at the original EIR and the difference is a modification gain or loss; at or above it, or with a qualitative factor, the old loan is derecognised and the new one recognised at its fair value with a new EIR.</li><li><b>Revised estimate</b> (B5.4.6): new expected flows under the same contract; the difference is the catch-up.</li><li><b>Contractual reset</b>: a change the contract provides for; a floating loan\'s monthly re-estimation already carries it, a fixed-rate loan takes the catch-up.</li><li><b>Correction</b> and <b>Not a change</b>: no accounting. An event dated on or before the take-on of 31 July 2024 is history and disclosure only.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>To confirm a restructure</h4><ol><li>Press <b>Find candidates</b> if the month\'s pack is new.</li><li>Filter the register, read the evidence and press the pencil on a candidate.</li><li>Choose the classification, give the reason and, for a modification, the Board approval, factors, fair value and fees; press <b>Propose</b>.</li><li>A colleague with eir.govern presses the tick.</li><li>Re-run the EIR revenue from the event\'s month with a reason and read the Accounting column.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>What happens next</h4><p>A confirmed modification, revised estimate or fixed-rate reset gets a schedule version and an accounting row that waits for the revenue run of its month. If the run cannot book it (for example a derecognition with no fair value recorded) the month stops with the reason, shown in Accounting, and later months wait. An approved negotiated rate move becomes a restructure candidate. The RBM return lists confirmed restructures with their Board approval. Every step is audit logged.</p>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li>"No candidate has been found yet. Press Find candidates at the top right to read the reschedule history (RS_02a to RS_02f) in the landing zone." (users without eir.govern read "A user who governs the EIR runs the search.")</li><li>"No event matches these filters." and "No rate move matches these filters."</li><li>"No rate move is recorded yet."</li><li>"Event #n proposed as modification. A different person approves it before it reaches the accounts." and "Event #n confirmed. Schedule version n is loaded; re-run the EIR revenue from (month) (with a reason) to book it."</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>"The person who proposed a classification cannot approve it."</b> Ask a colleague.</li><li><b>"This candidate has no effective date, so it cannot be booked as a ..."</b> Classify it as a correction or not a change, or obtain the date.</li><li><b>"E-Banker\'s schedule after the event is not on record ..."</b> The chart in force after the event must be landed first.</li><li><b>"A fair value must be more than nil and state its basis."</b></li><li><b>"The setting in force for rate_change_classification is \'Every rate change is a reset\'..."</b> The Governance Centre setting <b>Reset or modification</b> does not allow negotiated moves.</li><li><b>A move in a locked month is refused</b> with the locked-period message.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR & Revenue Recognition, Restructures & Resets.',
                'Press Find candidates (it can take a minute); nothing is confirmed by it.',
                'Filter the register, read the evidence and press the pencil on a candidate.',
                'Choose the classification and give the reason (for a modification also the Board approval, factors, fair value and fees), then press Propose.',
                'Ask a colleague with eir.govern to press the tick, or the cross with a reason.',
                'Re-run the EIR revenue from the event\'s month and read the Accounting column.',
                'On Rate resets, tick the moves, propose them as in-contract or negotiated with a reason, and have a colleague approve them.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-restructures' => 'Restructures & Resets: the candidate register with the engine\'s suggestion and the accounting booked'],
            'routes' => ['eir-restructures.index', 'eir-restructures.detect', 'eir-restructures.propose', 'eir-restructures.approve', 'eir-restructures.close',
                'eir-restructures.resets.propose', 'eir-restructures.resets.approve', 'eir-restructures.resets.close'],
        ],

        'Schedule review and approval' => [
            'body' => '<p>Schedule Review is where each contract\'s original repayment schedule (version 1) is generated from the contract master, compared with E-Banker\'s own schedule, and approved by a second person. The EIR is solved only on an approved version 1. In the sidebar, under <b>Financial modelling</b>, open <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b>, choose <b>EIR Data</b> and then the <b>Schedule Review</b> tab (breadcrumb: Financial modelling &gt; EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition &gt; EIR Data &gt; Schedule Review). The tab opens with <b>eir.view</b>; generating, approving and opening a schedule need the <b>settings</b> permission.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>Title <b>Schedule Review</b>, description "Generate the original schedule from Extract A, compare it with E-Banker\'s remaining rows (Extract B), then approve version 1", and the four tiles of EIR Data (<b>Contracts</b>, <b>Cash-flow rows</b>, <b>GL postings</b>, <b>Locked EIRs</b>).</li>'
                . '<li>The panel <b>Schedules by contract</b> with the comparison filter (<code>All comparison results</code>, <code>Within tolerance (n)</code>, <code>Cash variance (n)</code>, <code>Recalculated before any match (n)</code>, <code>No E-Banker schedule (n)</code>, <code>Not compared / blocked (n)</code>; it applies when you change it), the search box "Contract, account or reference" (contract number), <b>Search</b>, and <b>Clear</b> once a filter is set.</li>'
                . '<li>An amber bar with <b>Dry-run readiness</b>, <b>Generate eligible drafts</b> and the note "Extract B remaining rows are comparison evidence. Only an approved version-1 schedule is used by EIR."</li>'
                . '<li>The table: <b>Contract</b> (with the customer); <b>Extract A terms</b> (frequency and rate, the dates, any readiness issue in red, and in amber a tranche mismatch); <b>Generated</b> (rows in version 1); <b>Extract B remaining</b> (rows and "from" the cutoff date); <b>Comparison</b> (badge, "Cash variance ... on n instalment(s)", and "n after E-Banker recalculated from (date)"); <b>Approval</b>; and <b>Actions</b>. Fifteen contracts a page.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Statuses</h4><ul><li>Comparison: <b>WITHIN TOLERANCE</b> (green: the compared instalments agree on total cash within 1 percent of E-Banker\'s cash or MWK 1), <b>CASH VARIANCE</b> (red), <b>NOT COMPARABLE</b> (amber: E-Banker recalculated before any instalment could be matched), <b>NO REMAINING DATA</b> (amber: no E-Banker schedule) and <b>NOT GENERATED</b>.</li><li>Approval: <b>NOT GENERATED</b>, <b>DRAFT</b> (blue), <b>PENDING REVIEW</b> (amber) and <b>APPROVED</b> (green).</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Buttons and icons</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>Eye (<b>View schedule</b>): opens the loan schedule page (see The loan schedule page). Needs <b>settings</b>.</li>'
                . '<li>Gears (<b>Generate a draft schedule</b>): shown until the schedule is approved, or on an approved schedule with a tranche mismatch ("Generate again from the tranche register: ..."). Greyed out with "Not ready to generate: see the issues in the terms column" while the contract has readiness issues. Needs <b>settings</b>; you are recorded as the preparer.</li>'
                . '<li>Tick (<b>Approve version 1</b>): shown only while the approval is DRAFT. Opens <b>Approve contractual schedule</b>. Needs <b>settings</b>.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Dry-run readiness</b>: checks every contract and reports "Schedule dry run: X eligible; Y blocked." Nothing is written. Needs <b>settings</b>.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Generate eligible drafts</b>: generates a draft for every contract not yet approved that can be generated, recording you as the preparer, and reports "Generated N draft schedule(s); M skipped." Approved schedules are never overwritten. Needs <b>settings</b>.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>The approval window</h4><p><b>Approve contractual schedule</b>, "Review the comparison evidence before locking version 1.": the contract and its comparison badge, <b>Cash variance, instalments before any recalculation</b>, <b>Instalments compared / recalculated by E-Banker</b>, and <b>Review note</b> (up to 2000 characters). The note is required, marked with a red star, when the comparison is not within tolerance ("Required because the comparison is outside tolerance."), and optional otherwise. Buttons <b>Cancel</b> and <b>Approve version 1</b> ("Approving..." while it works).</p>'
                . '<h4>Who may approve</h4><p>The Governance Centre setting <b>Approving a version 1 schedule</b> decides. Under <b>Second person must approve</b> (the default) the person who generated the draft cannot approve it: "Maker-checker: the person who prepared the schedule cannot approve it (schedule_approval_control: Second person must approve)." Under <b>One person may draft and approve</b> the same person may do both. With no approved value the approval stops and names the setting.</p>'
                . '<h4>How a draft is generated</h4><p>The platform builds the instalments from the contract master: amount, contractual rate, payments per year, origination and maturity, moratorium and its type, and the instalment method of the scheme. Where the tranche register on Drawdowns shows the money went out on more than one date, the draft is built on the drawdowns: each tranche earns interest from its own date and the instalment is sized again over the instalments left. Where a partly drawn facility does not say whether interest is charged on the approved amount or on the balance, the platform reads it from the interest E-Banker posted, or the Governance Centre setting <b>Interest basis on a partly drawn facility</b> decides; with neither it refuses. A take-on loan keeps the schedule from the take-on workbook (source TAKEON_WORKBOOK); generating over it is refused and it arrives as PENDING REVIEW.</p>'
                . '<h4>How the comparison works</h4><p>Version 1 is the promise at origination. E-Banker regenerates its schedule at every rate reset and when arrears are spread over the instalments left, so only E-Banker\'s instalments before its first recalculation are held to version 1. The nth instalment is set against the nth, compared on total cash (E-Banker repays capitalised interest as principal), and a different due date is named as a cause.</p>'
                . '<h4>To approve a schedule</h4><ol><li>Filter or search for the contract and read the Comparison badge and cash variance.</li><li>Press the eye if you want to read the instalments side by side.</li><li>Press the tick, read the evidence and type a review note (required outside tolerance).</li><li>Press <b>Approve version 1</b>.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>What happens next</h4><p>An approved version 1 clears the blocker SCHEDULE_NOT_APPROVED and the contract can be solved on Calculations. The approver, time, comparison status and note are stored on the contract and written to the audit log ("EIR Schedule Approved", with the preparer).</p>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li>"No contracts found."</li><li>"Generated n draft rows for (contract)." and "Approved the version-1 schedule for (contract)."</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>Red text under Extract A terms</b> such as "Origination date is missing", "Maturity date is missing", "Drawn amount is not positive", "Contractual rate is missing or invalid", "Payment frequency is invalid", "Payment frequency was not stated by the source", "Maturity must be after origination", "Equity-excluded instrument" or "EIR is already locked", or a moratorium or partly drawn facility whose type is not stated: correct the source and load the contract master again.</li><li><b>"Approved schedule cannot be overwritten."</b> Only a schedule generated without the tranche register can be generated again while its EIR is not locked.</li><li><b>"The contract holds its version 1 schedule from TAKEON_WORKBOOK; a schedule is not generated over it."</b> A take-on schedule is approved as it stands.</li><li><b>"First repayment date falls after maturity."</b> The terms are inconsistent.</li><li><b>"Only a draft schedule can be approved."</b> and <b>"Generate or import an original schedule before approval."</b></li><li><b>"A review note is required when the generated schedule does not reconcile within tolerance to Extract B remaining cash flows."</b></li><li><b>The eye or gears answer with a rights error.</b> You hold eir.view but not settings.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR & Revenue Recognition, EIR Data, Schedule Review.',
                'Press Dry-run readiness to see how many contracts can be generated, then Generate eligible drafts.',
                'Ask a second person to filter or search for each contract and read the Comparison badge and cash variance.',
                'The second person presses the eye to read the instalments if needed, then the tick.',
                'They type a review note (required when the comparison is outside tolerance) and press Approve version 1.',
                'Check that the Approval column reads APPROVED, then solve the contract on EIR Calculations.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-schedule-review' => 'Schedule Review: generated against E-Banker remaining rows, with the comparison and approval'],
            'routes' => ['eir-schedules.dry-run', 'eir-schedules.generate-all', 'eir-schedules.generate', 'eir-schedules.approve'],
        ],

        'The loan schedule page' => [
            'body' => '<p>The loan schedule page shows one contract\'s generated version 1 schedule beside E-Banker\'s own schedule, instalment by instalment, so a reviewer can see why the two agree or differ before approving. Open it from <b>EIR Data</b>, <b>Schedule Review</b>, with the eye (<b>View schedule</b>) on the contract\'s row. It needs the <b>settings</b> permission.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>A trail at the top: <b>Schedule Review</b> (a link back) / the contract number.</li>'
                . '<li>Title <b>Loan Schedule</b> with the contract and the customer, a purple chip <b>Rate</b> with the contractual rate and, for a PLR-linked loan, a chip <b>Spread over prime x.xx pp</b> with its source (red with "drifts: held for review" when the spread drifts). Strapline: "Generated original schedule and E-Banker\'s own schedule (Extract B or the EMI chart) as evidence". A <b>Back to review</b> link at the right of the title.</li>'
                . '<li>Four tiles: <b>Approval status</b>, <b>Comparison</b>, <b>Cash variance (MWK)</b> ("before E-Banker recalculated") and <b>Instalments compared</b> ("compared / recalculated by E-Banker").</li><li>Under the tiles, a bar <b>EIR computation as at</b> with a date box and <b>View as at</b>.</li>'
                . '<li>Three page tabs with counts: <b>Instalment comparison</b> (opens first when there is anything to compare), <b>Generated version 1</b> and <b>E-Banker schedule</b>.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>The Instalment comparison tab</h4><p>Panel <b>Instalment by instalment</b>, explaining that only the instalments before E-Banker\'s first recalculation (date shown) are held to version 1 and that each instalment is compared on its total cash. Columns <b>#</b>, <b>Due date (generated / E-Banker)</b>, <b>Generated</b>, <b>E-Banker</b>, <b>Difference</b> (red when a compared instalment differs by more than MWK 1), <b>Compared?</b> (badge <b>Compared</b> in green, <b>E-Banker recalculated</b> in amber, <b>Generated only</b> or <b>E-Banker only</b> in grey) and <b>Why</b> (for example "Agrees", "Due dates differ", "Instalment differs: check the rate and how the instalment is sized", "Rate reset on (date) (compared in P5)", "Arrears spread over the instalments left (handled in P6)", "After E-Banker recalculated"). Fifteen rows a page.</p>'
                . '<h4>The two schedule tabs</h4><p><b>Generated version-1 schedule</b> ("Created from approved Extract A contract terms; this becomes usable by EIR only after approval.") and <b>E-Banker schedule (Extract B or EMI chart)</b> ("Forward-looking validation evidence from (cutoff); it does not replace version 1. Balances marked printed are E-Banker\'s own."). Columns <b>#</b>, <b>Due date</b>, <b>Opening balance</b>, <b>Interest capitalised</b> (interest added to the balance since the previous instalment, in amber; hover for the explanation), <b>Principal</b>, <b>Interest</b>, <b>Fees</b>, <b>Instalment</b>, <b>Closing balance</b> (with a small "printed" when the source printed it) and <b>Source</b>, and a totals row "Total, n rows" for the whole schedule. Fifteen rows a page.</p>'
                . '<h4>Buttons and icons</h4><ul><li>The date box and <b>View as at</b> (under the tiles): opens EIR as at a Date for this contract at the date chosen (yesterday by default).</li><li><b>Back to review</b> (a link beside the title): returns to Schedule Review.</li><li>The page tabs switch between the three views; nothing on this page changes a record.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li>"No instalments to compare yet. Generate the version-1 schedule from Schedule Review first."</li><li>"No schedule rows available." on an empty schedule tab.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>Most rows read E-Banker recalculated.</b> E-Banker regenerated its schedule early (a reset or arrears), so few instalments can be compared; the badge then reads NOT COMPARABLE and a review note explains it.</li><li><b>Generated only or E-Banker only at the end.</b> The two schedules have a different number of instalments: check the maturity and frequency on the contract master.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR Data, Schedule Review, and press the eye on the contract.',
                'Read the four tiles: approval, comparison, cash variance and instalments compared.',
                'On Instalment comparison, read the Compared? badge and the Why column for each instalment.',
                'Open Generated version 1 and E-Banker schedule to read the balances and any interest capitalised.',
                'Press View as at with a date to see the contract\'s EIR computation at that date, or Back to review.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-schedule-show' => 'One loan\'s schedule: the instalment comparison with generated and E-Banker totals'],
            'routes' => ['eir-schedules.show'],
        ],

        'Schedule intake: importing Extracts A, B and C and schedules' => [
            'body' => '<p>EIR Data Intake loads the files the EIR module runs on: the contract master (Extract A), repayment schedules, fees, contract transactions (Extract B), GL interest postings (Extract C), the reference-rate series and the drawdowns. The platform recognises the columns from its built-in aliases and any saved mapping, checks the whole file, and runs the import in the background. It has no menu entry: open <b>EIR Data</b> and press <b>EIR Data Intake</b> at the top right, or use <b>Import drawdowns</b>, <b>Import reference rates</b> or <b>Import fees</b> on those screens, which open it on the matching type. It needs the <b>settings</b> permission; a user with only <b>eir.fees.import</b> sees the fee type and nothing else.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>Title <b>EIR Data Intake</b>, strapline "Load contract terms, transactions, fees, drawdowns, reference rates and the interest the ledger posted", and a green badge <b>Schedules: n/m contracts</b> (contracts on the latest loan tape that have a repayment schedule).</li>'
                . '<li>Two tabs: <b>Upload</b> and <b>History</b> (with the number of uploads of the chosen type).</li>'
                . '<li>On Upload: the <b>How to import</b> panel for the chosen type (its steps and notes, with an X to close them and <b>Show the steps</b> to bring them back, <b>Download sample file</b>, <b>Download column guide</b>, a link to show the column table with each column\'s type, whether it is required, format and allowed values, description and example, and after a check the problems row by row, fifteen a page); the <b>Import type</b> list; the <b>File</b> drop area ("Choose a CSV or Excel file", "CSV, XLSX, XLS or ODS, up to 20 MB"); and <b>Import file</b> (greyed out until a file is chosen; "Importing..." while it works).</li>'
                . '<li>On History: the uploads of the chosen type with <b>File name</b>, <b>Status</b>, <b>Uploaded</b>, <b>Records inserted</b>, <b>Failed rows</b> and <b>Actions</b> (a download arrow for the failed rows file), and the line "n uploads of (type). The Imports screen logs every kind."</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>The import types and their required columns</h4><ul>'
                . '<li><b>Contract master (Extract A), facility terms, monthly</b>: <code>contract_id</code> only; every term is optional (origination, first repayment, maturity, approved and drawn amounts, rate and rate type, frequency, tenor, moratorium and its type, arrangement and legal fees, E-Banker scheme codes and others). A missing term does not reject the file; it appears later as a readiness issue.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Repayment schedule (per contract, once)</b>: <code>contract_id</code>, <code>due_date</code>, <code>principal_due</code>, <code>interest_due</code>; optional fee_due.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Fees and transaction costs</b> (Finance\'s fee workbook): <code>contract_id</code>, <code>fee_type</code>, <code>amount</code>, <code>transaction_date</code>; optional description, cashflow_direction, currency, source_system, source_reference, external_transaction_id, basis, gl_account_ref, integral. Give source_system and external_transaction_id so a re-import skips duplicates, and the fee GL in gl_account_ref (arrangement and legal fees default to 4873 and 4871). <code>integral</code> (yes or no) states the treatment: Finance includes its arrangement and legal fees in its EIR assessment, so its file says yes. Such a line arrives <b>Classified</b>, with the file named as the reason and the person who loads it as the classifier, and waits for a second person to review it on Fee Classification; a blank leaves the line pending. Loading the file again fills the treatment of a line still pending, never of one already classified or reviewed. <b>The file is proved against the GL fee postings before anything loads</b> (added 15 October 2026): every line must be accounted for by a posting the system holds (the same amount within the recon_tolerance band on its loan or naming its customer, or the same amount near the date; a fee posted in parts or with its corrections, and one posting paying two loans\' fees, are recognised), and every fee posting of the months the file covers must be in it (a posting and its reversal cancel). A line that repeats a fee the take-on workbook already holds for its loan (same type and amount, which would count it twice), or dated before the first GL fee posting, is refused; a later fee on a take-on loan is proved like any other. If any check fails, nothing loads: the import shows as failed and its exception file lists every reason. When the file ties, it loads, each held GL posting a line accounts for is tied to that line\'s loan (shown on Fee Classification with the file and row as the reason), and the exception file lists, as notes, the matches made on amount and date only, any difference within the band and any posting the system had tied to another loan.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Contract transactions (Extract B), scheduled and actual cash flows</b>: <code>customer_id</code>, <code>contract_id</code>, <code>sub_account_no</code>, <code>transaction_date</code>, <code>transaction_type</code>, <code>principal_component</code>, <code>interest_component</code>, <code>total_amount</code>, <code>scheduled_actual_flag</code>, <code>gl_posting_ref</code>. Scheduled rows are staged as E-Banker\'s remaining schedule for comparison and never replace version 1; actual rows are kept as transaction evidence. A blank fee component means no fee cash flow.</li>'
                . '<li><b>GL interest postings (Extract C), what the ledger posted</b>: <code>contract_id</code>, <code>period_year</code>, <code>period_month</code>, <code>interest_income_posted</code>.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Reference rates (File C), the prime lending rate by effective date</b>: <code>effective_date</code>, <code>rate</code> (see Reference rates and the spread over prime).</li>'
                . '<li><b>Drawdowns, one row per tranche paid out</b>: <code>contract_id</code>, <code>disbursement_date</code>, <code>amount</code> (see Drawdowns and undrawn commitments).</li></ul>'
                . '<p>Values are cleaned automatically: thousands separators and spaces are removed, bracketed amounts become negatives, Excel serial dates and dd-mm-yyyy or yyyy-mm-dd text are read as dates (except the reference rates and drawdowns, which accept year-first dates only), and the contract identifier is normalised. When you choose another type but upload an Extract B or reference-rate file, the screen says "(type) was recognised from the file\'s headers and selected for you."</p>'
                . '<h4>Buttons and icons</h4><ul><li><b>Import type</b>: chooses the type; the How to import panel follows it.</li><li><b>Download sample file</b> and <b>Download column guide</b>: a file in exactly the format the import reads (illustrative rows; identifiers start with 8 and names with Sample) and the same as an Excel workbook.</li><li><b>Import file</b>: reads the headers, checks the whole file and queues the job. If any row breaks a rule nothing is imported: "n problem(s) found in the file; nothing was imported. The rows are listed in the How to import panel above."</li><li><b>Open Import History</b>, <b>Download exception report</b> and <b>Download CSV</b>: the job in Import History and the contract-level exceptions.</li><li><b>Open Drawdowns</b> and <b>Open Reference Rates</b>: shown in those types\' results.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>While the import runs</h4><p>A status card moves from <b>Import queued successfully</b> to <b>Import processing</b> to <b>Import completed</b> or <b>Import failed</b>, with "This page checks progress automatically; you do not need to refresh it." and, at the end, <b>Rows processed</b>, <b>Rows inserted</b> and <b>Contract exceptions</b>.</p>'
                . '<h4>The Import result card</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>Contract master: <b>Contracts created</b>, <b>Terms updated</b>, <b>Unchanged</b>, and a tile counting the origination fees routed to Fee Classification as pending.</li>'
                . '<li>Repayment schedule: <b>Contracts loaded</b>, <b>Schedule rows</b>, <b>Held (not on tape)</b>, <b>Rejected</b>.</li>'
                . '<li>Fees: <b>Lines loaded as pending</b>, <b>Skipped (blank/zero)</b>, <b>Negative (netting) lines</b>, a tile counting unknown fee types loaded as other, "Totals by fee type in this file:", and the card <b>Fees against the fee GLs (4871 Legal, 4873 Arrangement)</b> (see Fee classification). That card compares the year with the trial balance; it reports and never blocks, and it can be switched off in the Governance Centre (<b>Fee imports checked against the fee GLs</b>). The proof against the individual GL postings described above always runs and does block.</li>'
                . '<li>Extract B: <b>Scheduled rows routed</b>, <b>Remaining rows staged</b>, <b>Rows not staged</b>, <b>Contracts staged</b>, then <b>New actual rows retained</b>, <b>Fee rows routed</b>, <b>Contracts held</b>, <b>Contracts rejected</b>; a red box "Scheduled rows were found, but none were staged." when every scheduled row failed.</li>'
                . '<li>Extract C: <b>Postings loaded</b>, <b>Annual totals excluded</b>, <b>GL restatements applied</b>, <b>Negative rows (check sign)</b>, <b>Total interest posted</b>, and "Interest posted by period" to check against the GL.</li>'
                . '<li>Reference rates: <b>Rates loaded</b>, <b>Already stored</b>, <b>Rate changes in the file</b>, <b>Rows repeating the previous rate</b> and the rate now in force.</li>'
                . '<li>Drawdowns: <b>Drawdowns loaded</b>, <b>Already stored</b>, <b>Repeated rows in the file</b>, <b>Facilities in the file</b>, <b>Total drawn in the file</b>, and the date range.</li>'
                . '<li>Every type: "n contract-level exception(s)" with <b>Contract</b>, <b>Status</b> (<code>held</code>, <code>rejected</code>, <code>incomplete</code> or <code>restated</code>) and <b>Reason</b>, and "Schedule coverage is now n/m contracts on the latest loan tape."</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>What happens next</h4><p>Each import appears in Import History named "EIR (type): (file name)" and writes an audit entry. Extract A creates or updates the contract profiles; fees land in Fee Classification as Pending with any rule suggestion; Extract B remaining rows feed the Schedule Review comparison and actual rows the revenue run; Extract C feeds GL Reconciliation.</p>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li>"No (type) file has been imported yet. Use the Upload tab to import the first one." on History.</li><li>"The file was not imported" with the reason.</li><li>"Could not refresh the status just now. Retrying automatically."</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>"The file is missing required columns: ... Check that the correct source type and file were selected."</b> Rename the columns to the field names above or ask the administrator for a mapping.</li><li><b>Status "held" with reason "not on tape".</b> The contract is not on the latest loan book; its rows are parked until it is.</li><li><b>Status "incomplete".</b> The contract lacks a term the solver needs; Schedule Review shows which.</li><li><b>Status "restated".</b> A GL figure replaced one for a period that may already have been reconciled.</li><li><b>Import failed.</b> Open Import History and download the exception report.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR Data and press EIR Data Intake (or an import button on Drawdowns, Reference Rates or Fee Classification).',
                'Choose the Import type that matches the file and read the How to import panel; download the sample file if needed.',
                'Choose the CSV or Excel file (up to 20 MB) in the File area.',
                'Press Import file; if any row breaks a rule nothing is imported and the problems are listed by row.',
                'Watch the status card move to Import completed; the page refreshes itself.',
                'Read the Import result tiles and the contract-level exceptions; press Download CSV for the detail.',
                'Load in month-end order: contract master, then fees, schedules and drawdowns, then Extract B, then Extract C.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-intake' => 'EIR Data Intake: the import type, the How to import panel and the coverage badge'],
            'routes' => ['eir-intake.index', 'eir-intake.analyze', 'eir-intake.save-template', 'eir-intake.import', 'eir-intake.status', 'eir-intake.sample'],
        ],

        'Accounting rules' => [
            'body' => '<p>Accounting Rules is the rulebook that suggests whether an imported fee or cost is integral to the EIR. An integral fee is part of making the loan (an arrangement fee, a direct legal cost) and belongs in the yield; a non-integral fee is charged for something else (a monitoring service, a penalty) and stays out. Rules only suggest: every imported line stays pending until it is classified and reviewed by someone else. In the sidebar, under <b>Financial modelling</b>, open <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b>, then <b>Accounting Rules</b> (breadcrumb: Financial modelling &gt; EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition &gt; Accounting Rules). The screen and every action on it need the <b>settings</b> permission.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>Title <b>EIR Accounting Rules</b>, strapline "Approved rules that suggest whether an imported fee or cost forms part of the EIR".</li>'
                . '<li>The panel <b>Rule register</b> with the line "n rule(s), m approved. The lowest priority number is tried first. Rules only suggest: every imported line stays pending until it is classified and reviewed by someone else."</li>'
                . '<li>Columns: <b>Priority / rule</b> (the priority, the rule name and an <b>Active</b> or <b>Inactive</b> badge), <b>Matching criteria</b> ("Type:", "Description:", "Direction:" and "GL:", each <code>any</code> or <code>either</code> when blank), <b>Treatment</b> (an <b>Integral</b> or <b>Non-integral</b> badge with the rationale beneath), <b>Approval</b> (<b>Approved</b> in green with the approver\'s name, or <b>Pending approval</b> in amber) and <b>Actions</b>. Rules are listed by priority, then name.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Buttons and icons</h4><ul>'
                . '<li><b>Create rule</b> (above the table): opens <b>Create accounting rule</b>.</li>'
                . '<li>Pencil (<b>Edit rule</b>): opens <b>Edit accounting rule</b>. Saving any change resets the approval.</li>'
                . '<li>Tick (<b>Approve rule</b>): shown only while the rule is pending approval.</li>'
                . '<li>No-entry circle (<b>Disable rule</b>) on an active rule, or tick (<b>Enable rule</b>) on an inactive one.</li></ul>'
                . '<p>All of them need <b>settings</b>. The screen records who approved a rule but does not stop the author of a rule approving it; MAIIC\'s control policy should require a second person.</p>'
                . '<h4>The rule window</h4><p>"Define the conditions and suggested IFRS 9 treatment." Two sections, <b>Rule identity</b> and <b>Matching conditions</b> ("Blank conditions match any value."):</p><ul><li><b>Rule name</b> (required, up to 255 characters; "e.g. Direct arrangement fees").</li><li><b>Priority</b> (required, 1 to 65535, default 100). Lower numbers are tried first.</li><li><b>Fee/cost type</b>: <code>Any type</code>, <code>arrangement</code>, <code>legal</code>, <code>appraisal</code>, <code>default</code>, <code>levy</code> or <code>other</code>.</li><li><b>Description contains</b>: text that must appear in the fee description, ignoring capitals.</li><li><b>GL account</b>: must match the fee line\'s GL reference exactly.</li><li><b>Cash direction</b>: <code>Either</code>, <code>Received by MAIIC</code> or <code>Paid by MAIIC</code>.</li><li><b>Proposed treatment</b>: <code>Integral - include in EIR</code> or <code>Non-integral - exclude from EIR</code>.</li><li><b>Accounting rationale</b> (required, up to 2000 characters).</li><li><b>Make this rule active</b>.</li></ul><p>Footer: <b>Cancel</b> and <b>Create rule</b> or <b>Update rule</b> ("Saving..." while it works). The first validation error shows in red in the window.</p>'
                . '<h4>How a rule is matched</h4><p>Only rules that are both Active and Approved take part. They are tried in priority order and the first rule whose every filled-in condition matches the fee line is used. A rule with all conditions blank matches everything, so give it a high priority number as the catch-all.</p>'
                . '<h4>To add and approve a rule</h4><ol><li>Press <b>Create rule</b>, fill in the window and press <b>Create rule</b>.</li><li>A reviewer reads the criteria and rationale and presses the tick.</li><li>On Fee Classification press <b>Re-apply rules to pending lines</b> so lines already on file pick up the suggestion.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>What happens next</h4><p>New fee lines imported after approval carry the rule\'s name and treatment in the <b>Rule suggestion</b> column of Fee Classification. Lines already pending change only when someone presses <b>Re-apply rules to pending lines</b>; classified or reviewed lines are never changed by a rule. Every create, update, approve and toggle is written to the audit log.</p>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li>"No rules created yet. Use Create rule at the top right to add the first one."</li><li>"Accounting rule created and awaiting approval.", "Rule updated; approval has been reset.", "Accounting rule approved.", "Rule activated." and "Rule deactivated."</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>A rule is approved but lines show "No matching rule".</b> The rule is inactive, a condition does not match (a GL account must match exactly), or the lines were imported before approval: press Re-apply rules to pending lines.</li><li><b>A red message in the window.</b> Usually a blank name or rationale, or a priority below 1.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR & Revenue Recognition, Accounting Rules.',
                'Press Create rule and enter the Rule name and a Priority (lower numbers are tried first).',
                'Set the matching conditions; leave a condition blank to match any value.',
                'Choose the Proposed treatment, write the Accounting rationale and tick Make this rule active.',
                'Press Create rule. The message reads "Accounting rule created and awaiting approval."',
                'Ask a reviewer to press the tick on the row, then press Re-apply rules to pending lines on Fee Classification.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-rules' => 'EIR Accounting Rules: the rule register with priority, criteria, treatment and approval'],
            'routes' => ['eir-accounting-rules.index', 'eir-accounting-rules.store', 'eir-accounting-rules.update', 'eir-accounting-rules.approve', 'eir-accounting-rules.toggle'],
        ],

        'Fee classification' => [
            'body' => '<p>Fee Classification is the work queue where every imported fee or cost line is decided integral or non-integral, with a written reason, and then reviewed by a second person. Until every line on a loan is reviewed (or rejected) the loan cannot be solved: readiness reports FEE_CLASSIFICATION_PENDING. In the sidebar, under <b>Financial modelling</b>, open <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b>, then <b>Fee Classification</b> (breadcrumb: Financial modelling &gt; EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition &gt; Fee Classification). The screen opens with <b>settings</b> or <b>eir.fees.import</b>; classifying, reviewing, returning and re-applying rules need <b>settings</b>; tying a held GL fee line needs <b>eir.govern</b>.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>Title <b>Fee and Cost Classification</b>, strapline "Classify imported fee and cost lines, then have someone else review them before the EIR is calculated".</li>'
                . '<li>Four tiles: <b>Pending lines</b>, <b>Classified lines</b>, <b>Reviewed lines</b> and <b>Rejected lines</b>, each the number of lines with their total amount beneath.</li>'
                . '<li>Under the tiles, <b>Import fees</b> and <b>Re-apply rules to pending lines</b>, then one row of tabs: <b>Fee lines</b> (with the number of lines), <b>Held GL fee lines</b>, <b>Source differences</b>, <b>Overlaps to decide</b> and <b>Fees against the GL</b>. The open tab is kept in the address. On <b>Fee lines</b>: the strip <b>Accounting rulebook</b>: "n approved rule(s), m awaiting approval. x of y pending line(s) carry a matched rule." (or "No pending lines."), and in amber "n pending line(s) have no matched rule: re-apply after approving rules, or classify them manually."</li>'
                . '<li>The filter bar: <b>Contract or account</b> ("Part of the number"), <b>Status</b> (<code>All statuses</code>, Pending, Classified, Reviewed, Rejected), <b>Fee or cost type</b> (<code>All types</code>, arrangement, legal, appraisal, default, levy, other), <b>GL</b> (for example 4874) and <b>Apply filters</b>. The dropdowns apply as soon as they change; the contract and GL boxes apply on Enter or Apply filters. Superseded lines (another source holds the same fee) are hidden unless <b>Include superseded lines</b> is ticked, and the four tiles count only the lines still in play. Above the table, <b>Select all lines matching these filters</b> ticks every line the filters select, on every page, not only the fifteen shown; superseded lines are left out, and so are reviewed lines unless the status filter is Reviewed. The selection bar then reads "all lines matching the filters, on every page", with <b>Clear selection</b>, and the classify, review and return buttons act on all of them.</li>'
                . '<li>The selection bar, once lines are ticked: "n selected line(s)", "n without a matched rule", and the action buttons.</li>'
                . '<li>The table: a tick box (the header box selects every unreviewed line on the page), <b>Contract / reference</b> (with a grey <b>Superseded by (source)</b> badge on a line kept but left out of the EIR because another source holds the contract\'s fees), <b>Fee or cost</b> (type, description and direction, or "direction pending"), <b>Amount</b>, <b>Rule suggestion</b> (rule name and an Integral or Non-integral badge, or "No matching rule"), <b>Decision</b> (the recorded treatment and reason) and <b>Status</b> (<b>Pending</b> amber, <b>Classified</b> blue, <b>Reviewed</b> green, <b>Rejected</b> red). Pending first, then Classified, then the rest, newest first, fifteen a page.</li></ul>'
                . '<li>The tab <b>Fees against the GL</b>: the panel <b>Fees against the fee GLs (4871 Legal, 4873 Arrangement)</b>, with the Governance Centre setting in force at the right. It has one row a year, newest first: <b>Year</b>, <b>Fees imported</b>, <b>GL movement</b>, <b>Difference</b>, <b>Status</b> (<b>Agrees</b>, <b>Difference</b>, <b>Trial balance incomplete</b>) and a <b>GLs</b> link that opens the year to its GLs, each with a <b>Months</b> link. The years with no trial balance loaded at all share one grey row (for example "2020 to 2024", <b>No trial balance</b>), because their fees cannot be checked.</li>'
                . '<li>The tabs <b>Held GL fee lines</b> (fee postings on the fee income GLs whose batch names no single loan; columns <b>Date</b>, <b>Batch</b>, <b>Amount</b>, <b>GL</b>, <b>Narration</b>, <b>Why it is held</b>, <b>Loan the evidence points to</b> and, for eir.govern, <b>Action</b>), <b>Source differences</b> (where another fee source carries a different amount from the source of record; columns <b>Loan</b>, <b>Fee type</b>, <b>Source of record</b>, <b>Amount of record</b>, <b>Other source</b>, <b>Other amount</b>, <b>Difference</b>) and <b>Overlaps to decide</b> (a fee line of a lower source that matches no fee of the source of record in a month that source covers, where the two sources do not total the same for the fee event; the loan\'s EIR waits until a person with eir.govern presses <b>A fee of its own</b> or <b>The same fee</b> and gives the reason).</li>'
                . '<h4>Buttons and icons</h4><ul>'
                . '<li><b>Import fees</b> (under the tiles): opens EIR Data Intake on the fee type.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Re-apply rules to pending lines</b> (under the tiles, settings): matches the approved rulebook against every pending line again.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Classify using each line\'s rule</b> (greyed out when no selected line has a rule): each line takes the treatment of its own matched rule; lines with no rule are skipped, not guessed.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Classify manually</b>: one treatment for every selected line.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Review/approve</b>: opens <b>Approve classifications</b>.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Return to classification</b> (greyed out unless classified or reviewed lines are selected): sends them back to Pending with a reason.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Move to another loan</b> (under the contract on each line, eir.govern): for a line loaded against the wrong loan, for example a source file whose rows carried each other\'s fees. Give the loan, the right date if the file had that wrong too, and the reason (written to the audit log). Neither loan\'s EIR may be locked: reopen it first. The line goes back to Pending, to be classified and reviewed again on its new loan, and the fee source precedence is applied again.</li>'
                . '<li>Paperclip (<b>Tie this fee line to its loan</b>, Held GL fee lines, eir.govern): opens <b>Tie the fee line to its loan</b>.</li>'
                . '<li>Ban icon (<b>Not this loan\'s fee</b>, Held GL fee lines, eir.govern, shown where the system names a likely loan): asks the reason and records that the posting is not that loan\'s fee. The posting stays held for the right loan; the named loan is no longer stopped by it. While such a posting is unresolved, the named loan\'s EIR can be neither solved nor locked (blocker <b>A held GL fee posting names the loan as its likely owner</b>, added 15 October 2026).</li>'
                . '<li><b>GLs</b> / <b>Hide GLs</b> and <b>Months</b> / <b>Hide months</b> on the fee GL panel.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>The windows</h4><ul>'
                . '<li><b>Classify by rule, n line(s)</b> or <b>Classify manually, n line(s)</b>: in manual mode <b>EIR treatment</b> (<code>Choose treatment</code>, <code>Integral - include in EIR</code>, <code>Non-integral - exclude</code>) and <b>Accounting rule</b> (<code>No linked rule</code> or an approved rule to cite); when any selected line has no direction, <b>Cash-flow direction for the n line(s) loaded without one</b> (<code>Leave blank (the line cannot enter the EIR)</code>, <code>Received: a fee charged to the borrower</code>, <code>Paid: a cost MAIIC paid</code>); and <b>Accounting reason</b>. In rule mode the rule name is added to the reason as "[applied by rule: name]". Buttons <b>Cancel</b> and <b>Save classification</b>.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Approve classifications</b>: "Approve the classifications for n selected line(s)? You cannot review decisions that you classified yourself." Buttons <b>Cancel</b> and <b>Approve classifications</b>.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Return to classification, n line(s)</b>: explains that the lines go back to Pending and that a line of a contract whose EIR is locked cannot be returned until that EIR is reopened; <b>Reason</b> (at least 5 characters). Buttons <b>Cancel</b> and <b>Return to classification</b>.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Tie the fee line to its loan</b>: the amount, GL, date and batch; <b>Loan (contract or account number)</b> (filled with the loan the evidence points to, with the loans in the batch listed); <b>Reason (written to the audit log)</b>, at least 10 characters. Buttons <b>Cancel</b> and <b>Tie to the loan</b>.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Who may review</h4><p>The reviewer must not be the classifier: "A classifier cannot review their own decision." An administrator may review their own lines only while the Governance Centre setting <b>Administrator override of maker-checker</b> is <b>Allowed for administrators</b>; the audit entry then carries maker_checker_override. An integral line with no cash-flow direction cannot be reviewed: return it so the classifier states whether the fee was received or paid.</p>'
                . '<h4>To classify and review</h4><ol><li>Filter by status Pending and press <b>Apply filters</b>.</li><li>Tick the lines and press <b>Classify using each line\'s rule</b> or <b>Classify manually</b>.</li><li>Give the treatment (manual), the direction if asked, and the reason; press <b>Save classification</b>.</li><li>A second person filters by Classified, ticks the lines and presses <b>Review/approve</b>, then <b>Approve classifications</b>.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>Where the fee lines come from, and which one counts</h4><p>Besides EIR Data Intake, fee lines arrive from the E-Banker GL fee postings (fee GLs 4871, 4873, 4874 and 4875, tied to their loan through the batch; a posting whose batch names no single loan is held for a person to tie), from the E-Banker loan charges (P2_07) and from the take-on workbook. One fee, one source: the Governance Centre setting <b>Which source a contract\'s fees come from</b> decides which source is the source of record for a contract. Each other source is compared with it fee event by fee event: where its lines total the same money (even split into different fee types) they are marked superseded, kept and shown, and never enter the EIR; where an amount differs it is listed under <b>Source differences</b>; where a line matches nothing it waits under <b>Overlaps to decide</b>. A source is the source of record only for the months it covers: the GL fee build records the months of the GL fee postings, and each Finance fee workbook load records the months its file accounts for, so a line dated in a month the source of record does not cover stands. Every line still arrives Pending.</p>'
                . '<h4>What happens next</h4><p>Reviewed integral lines adjust the loan\'s initial net investment: received amounts reduce it, paid amounts increase it. Once every line on a loan is Reviewed or Rejected the fee blocker clears. Every action writes an event on the fee line and an audit entry ("EIR Fees Classified", "EIR Fee Classifications Reviewed", "EIR Fee Rules Re-applied"). Classifying a line again before review replaces the decision and clears any earlier reviewer.</p>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li>"No fee or cost lines match these filters. Clear a filter to see more." and "No fee or cost lines have been imported yet. Load fees through EIR Data Intake, then classify them here."</li><li>"No fee posting is held: every GL fee leg was placed on its loan, or the GL fee build has not been run." and "No difference between fee sources: ..."</li><li>"n fee/cost line(s) classified." and "n classification(s) approved."</li><li>"Re-applied the rulebook to n pending line(s): a matched, b still unmatched, c suggestion(s) updated." or "No pending fee lines to re-match."</li><li>"n line(s) returned to classification. Classify them again; a second person then reviews them."</li><li>"The fee line is tied to loan n. It now waits on the list below for its classification and review."</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>"A classifier cannot review their own decision."</b> Ask a colleague.</li><li><b>"n integral line(s) have no cash-flow direction (...). Return them to classification ...; nothing was approved."</b></li><li><b>"No line could be classified: none of the selected lines has a matching rule. Re-apply the rulebook, or classify them manually."</b></li><li><b>"Enter the accounting reason."</b>, <b>"Choose a treatment."</b> and <b>"Enter the reason."</b></li><li><b>"The original EIR of n is locked, so its fee lines are part of it: reopen the EIR first. Nothing was returned."</b></li><li><b>A user with eir.fees.import sees no tick boxes.</b> Classifying needs settings.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR & Revenue Recognition, Fee Classification.',
                'Set the filters (status Pending, a fee type, a GL or a contract) and press Apply filters; to act on every page, press Select all lines matching these filters.',
                'Tick the lines and press Classify using each line\'s rule, or Classify manually.',
                'Give the treatment (manual mode), the cash-flow direction if asked, and the Accounting reason; press Save classification.',
                'A second person filters by Classified, ticks the lines, presses Review/approve and then Approve classifications.',
                'Tie any held GL fee line to its loan with the paperclip, and read the fee GL card for differences.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-fees' => 'Fee and Cost Classification: status tiles, the tab row and the Fee lines tab'],
            'routes' => ['eir-fee-classification.index', 'eir-fee-classification.rematch', 'eir-fee-classification.classify', 'eir-fee-classification.review', 'eir-fee-classification.return', 'eir-fee-classification.tie-held'],
        ],

        'EIR calculations, approval and reopening' => [
            'body' => '<p>Calculations solves each contract\'s original effective interest rate, records the evidence, and lets a second person approve and lock it. The solver finds the single rate at which the version 1 cash flows, discounted to origination, equal the initial net investment (the amount advanced less integral fees received plus integral costs paid). In the sidebar, under <b>Financial modelling</b>, open <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b>, choose <b>EIR Calculations</b> and then <b>Calculations</b> (breadcrumb: Financial modelling &gt; EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition &gt; EIR Calculations &gt; Calculations). The screen and its actions need the <b>settings</b> permission.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>Title <b>EIR Calculations</b>, strapline "Calculate each contract\'s original effective interest rate, then have it approved and locked by someone else".</li>'
                . '<li>Five tiles: <b>Pending</b> ("not calculated yet (see Blockers)"), <b>Reopened</b> ("reopened for recalculation"), <b>Blocked</b> ("stopped by a calculation run"), <b>Calculated</b> ("awaiting approval") and <b>Locked</b> ("approved and locked").</li>'
                . '<li>A red panel <b>Locked EIRs to review</b> when a locked EIR has fee evidence that was not in the inputs it was locked on: a held posting naming the loan, or a fee recorded after the lock (found by the system), or evidence a person raised (<code>php artisan eir:lock-review</code>). Each line shows the loan, the rate it was locked at, the reason and when it was raised. The system never reopens anything: the circular-arrows icon requests a reopening (approved by a second person, after which the flag closes), and the tick clears the flag with the reason, by a person other than the one who raised it (added 15 October 2026).</li>'
                . '<li>The filter bar: <b>Contract</b> ("Contract, account or reference"; matches the contract, sub-account, GL code, reference, portfolio or product), <b>Status</b> (<code>All statuses</code> and the five statuses), <b>Apply filters</b> ("Applying..."), <b>Clear filters</b>, the line "Showing a to b of n matching contracts." (or "No contracts match."), a <b>Filters active</b> badge, and the bulk approval line "n calculated contract(s) can be approved across all pages." followed by "Administrator override is active." or "x calculated by you and y without maker evidence will be skipped."</li>'
                . '<li>The table: a tick box (none on locked rows; the header box selects every unlocked row on the page), <b>Contract</b> (currency beneath), <b>Status</b> (badge, with the blocking message in red beneath), <b>Periodic EIR</b>, <b>Effective annual EIR</b>, <b>Evidence</b> ("Calculated" date and time, solver method and iterations, "Locked" date and time) and <b>Actions</b>. Blocked rows first, then Reopened, Calculated, Pending and Locked, most recently changed first, fifteen a page.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Buttons and icons</h4><ul>'
                . '<li><b>Calculate selected (n)</b> (above the table, once rows are ticked): queues the ticked contracts for calculation; you are recorded as the maker.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Approve and lock all eligible (n)</b> (above the table, when any contract is Calculated): asks "Approve and lock n EIRs?" ("All n eligible calculated EIRs across every page are locked permanently. This cannot be undone.") and locks every calculated contract you did not calculate. Greyed out when none is eligible.</li>'
                . '<li>Calculator: on every unlocked row; its tip reads <b>Calculate</b> (Pending), <b>Retry calculation</b> (Blocked) or <b>Recalculate</b> (Calculated or Reopened). A recalculation archives the earlier result.</li>'
                . '<li>Padlock (<b>Approve and lock</b>): on a Calculated row. Asks "Approve and lock (contract)?" ("The original EIR is locked permanently. Only an administrator can reopen it."). Refused for the person who calculated it.</li>'
                . '<li>Open padlock (<b>Reopen locked EIR</b>): on a locked row, shown only to a user with the administrator role while the Governance Centre setting <b>Administrator override of maker-checker</b> is <b>Allowed for administrators</b>. Everyone else sees "Locked, an administrator must reopen it".</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>The reopen window</h4><p><b>Reopen locked original EIR</b> with the contract, an amber warning "The locked calculation will be archived. Revenue-recognition rows will be superseded and discounted ECL will be marked stale. The EIR must then be recalculated and independently approved.", <b>Reason for reopening</b> (at least 10 characters, up to 500, "recorded in audit history"; placeholder "Describe the source-data error or controlled correction."), and <b>Cancel</b> and <b>Reopen and invalidate results</b>.</p>'
                . '<h4>Status values</h4><ul><li><b>Pending</b>: never calculated. The Blockers tab says why one cannot be calculated yet.</li><li><b>Calculated</b>: solved, awaiting approval.</li><li><b>Blocked</b>: the readiness check or the solver failed; the reason is under the badge.</li><li><b>Locked</b>: approved by a second person; permanent unless reopened.</li><li><b>Reopened</b>: the rate has been cleared and must be solved and approved again.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Readiness</h4><p>Before solving, the contract is checked. The codes are listed in plain English on the Blockers tab (see Coverage and blockers); besides those, a take-on loan can show <b>TAKEON_NOT_RECOMPUTED</b> or <b>TAKEON_SOLVE_REFUSED</b>: the take-on build checks that the workbook with no fee solves to the rates its own lines charge (Governance Centre setting <b>Take-on EIR: the check before it is used</b>) and refuses a block that fails, with the reason. A facility drawn in tranches can show <b>SCHEDULE_BEFORE_FIRST_DRAWDOWN</b> or <b>SCHEDULE_NOT_ON_TRANCHES</b>: generate its schedule again on Schedule Review.</p>'
                . '<h4>Facilities drawn in tranches and take-on loans</h4><p>A facility signed before any money went out is solved from its first drawdown, with each later tranche paid out on its own date; a fee debited in the drawdown batch is part of that drawdown. A take-on loan is solved on the cash flows the take-on build read from the workbook, with the workbook\'s fees once reviewed, and is rolled forward from August 2024 from its balance at the take-on of 31 July 2024.</p>'
                . '<h4>To calculate and lock</h4><ol><li>Filter by status Pending (or Reopened) and press <b>Apply filters</b>.</li><li>Tick the contracts and press <b>Calculate selected (n)</b>.</li><li>Refresh after a moment: each row reads Calculated with its rates and evidence, or Blocked with the reason.</li><li>A second person checks the effective annual EIR and presses the padlock (or <b>Approve and lock all eligible</b>).</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>What a lock means, and what reopening invalidates</h4><p>A locked EIR is the loan\'s original rate for its life: it drives the monthly revenue run and the discount rate of the loan\'s ECL. Reopening archives the locked calculation with your reason, moves every revenue row of the loan to history, clears the loan\'s discounted ECL (status STALE_EIR_REOPENED) and sets the status to Reopened. The audit entry "Locked Original EIR Reopened" records the old rate and the reason. A reopen is refused when any month the loan\'s roll-forward or discounted ECL reached is a locked period; unlock that month first, with its reason.</p>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li>"No contracts match these filters. Clear the filters to see every contract." and "No contracts are loaded yet. Load the contract master through EIR Data Intake first."</li><li>"n contract(s) queued for EIR calculation."</li><li>"Original EIR for (contract) approved and locked."</li><li>"n EIR(s) approved and permanently locked." with "m contract(s) were skipped by maker-checker or approval validation." when some were.</li><li>"Original EIR for (contract) reopened. Recalculate and obtain independent approval before reuse."</li><li>Refusals show in a red box headed "The action could not be completed".</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>"The calculator cannot approve and lock their own EIR."</b> A different person must approve.</li><li><b>"The calculation has no identifiable maker and cannot be approved."</b> Recalculate it so a maker is recorded.</li><li><b>"Only a successfully calculated EIR can be approved and locked."</b></li><li><b>"There are no calculated, unlocked EIRs to approve."</b> and <b>"No EIRs were locked. n contract(s) failed maker-checker or approval validation."</b></li><li><b>"A locked original EIR cannot be recalculated."</b> Reopen it first.</li><li><b>"Only an administrator can reopen a locked original EIR."</b> and <b>"A specific reopening reason of at least 10 characters is required."</b></li><li><b>Red text under a Blocked badge.</b> Fix the named issue (intake, Schedule Review or Fee Classification), then press the calculator to retry.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR & Revenue Recognition, EIR Calculations, Calculations.',
                'Filter by status Pending (or Reopened) and press Apply filters.',
                'Tick the contracts to solve and press Calculate selected.',
                'Refresh after a moment: each row reads Calculated with its rates and solver evidence, or Blocked with the reason.',
                'A second person checks the effective annual EIR and presses the padlock on each row, or Approve and lock all eligible, and confirms.',
                'To correct a locked EIR, an administrator (with the override allowed) presses the open padlock and gives the reason.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-calculations' => 'EIR Calculations: the five status tiles, filters and the contract table with its action icons'],
            'routes' => ['eir-calculations.index', 'eir-calculations.calculate', 'eir-calculations.approve-all', 'eir-calculations.approve', 'eir-calculations.reopen'],
        ],

        'Coverage and blockers' => [
            'body' => '<p>The Blockers, Coverage by Portfolio and Largest Facilities tabs answer one portfolio-level question: how much of the loan book carries a locked EIR, and what is stopping the rest. They count contracts and weigh them by carrying amount, because a blocker on a handful of large facilities matters more than one on many small ones. In the sidebar, under <b>Financial modelling</b>, open <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b>, choose <b>EIR Calculations</b> and then <b>Blockers</b>, <b>Coverage by Portfolio</b> or <b>Largest Facilities</b> (breadcrumb: Financial modelling &gt; EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition &gt; EIR Calculations &gt; Blockers). They need the <b>eir.view</b> permission and only read.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on every tab</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>The title of the tab with its description: <b>Blockers</b> ("What is holding back the contracts that do not yet carry a locked EIR, ranked by exposure"), <b>Coverage by Portfolio</b> ("How much of each portfolio carries a locked EIR") or <b>Largest Facilities</b> ("The largest facilities by exposure, their EIR state, and what still blocks those not yet locked").</li>'
                . '<li>Above the table, the <b>Reporting period</b> list (months on the loan book, newest first) and the <b>Portfolio</b> list (<code>All portfolios</code> or one). Each applies as soon as you change it.</li>'
                . '<li>Seven tiles: <b>Contract coverage</b> (percentage, a bar and "n of m locked"), <b>Exposure coverage</b> (percentage, a bar and the covered against the in-scope carrying amount; hover for the full figures), then <b>Locked</b>, <b>Awaiting approval</b>, <b>Ready to solve</b>, <b>Blocked</b> and <b>Out of scope</b>, each a count with its exposure. A coverage percentage is green at 90 percent or more, amber at 50 or more, otherwise red.</li>'
                . '<li>When exposure coverage runs more than one and a half times ahead of contract coverage, the line "Covered facilities are larger than average: ..."</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>The Blockers tab</h4><p>Panel <b>Blockers, ranked by exposure</b> ("Ranked by carrying amount, not count. Sole blocker counts the contracts this alone holds back."). Columns <b>Blocker</b> (plain-English label with the code beneath), <b>Contracts</b>, <b>Exposure</b>, <b>% of book</b>, <b>Sole blocker</b> and <b>Facilities</b> (an eye, "Show the facilities held back by this blocker", which opens Largest Facilities narrowed to that blocker; on the selected blocker it becomes a circled cross, "Clear this blocker filter"). Beneath, when there are any, <b>Out of scope, not blockers</b>: contracts outside the EIR engine by decision (<b>Mega Farm programme, out of the EIR\'s scope (D30)</b>; <b>Equity instrument, outside the EIR engine</b>), with <b>Reason</b>, <b>Contracts</b> and <b>Exposure</b>, left out of the coverage figures.</p>'
                . '<h4>The Coverage by Portfolio tab</h4><p>Panel <b>Coverage by portfolio</b>: <b>Portfolio</b>, <b>Contracts</b>, <b>Covered</b>, <b>Coverage</b> (coloured as above) and <b>Exposure</b>; contracts without a portfolio appear as Unassigned.</p>'
                . '<h4>The Largest Facilities tab</h4><p>Panel <b>Largest affected facilities</b> (with ", blocker CODE" when narrowed) and "The n largest by exposure of m. Use the eye on the Blockers tab to narrow to one blocker." At most 50 are listed, fifteen a page. Columns <b>Contract</b> ("Not on the current tape" in amber when the loan has an EIR profile but is missing from the month\'s loan book), <b>Portfolio</b>, <b>Exposure</b>, <b>State</b> (<b>Locked</b>, <b>Awaiting approval</b>, <b>Ready to solve</b>, <b>Blocked</b>, <b>Out of scope</b>) and <b>Blockers</b> (the codes, or the out-of-scope reason). <b>Clear blocker filter</b> removes the narrowing.</p>'
                . '<h4>Blocker codes in plain English</h4><ul><li><b>CONTRACT_PROFILE_MISSING</b>: No EIR contract profile exists.</li><li><b>ORIGINATION_DATE_MISSING</b>: Origination/value date is missing.</li><li><b>DRAWN_AMOUNT_MISSING</b>: Drawn amount is missing or zero.</li><li><b>FREQUENCY_INVALID</b>: Payment frequency is not a supported value.</li><li><b>FREQUENCY_ASSUMED</b>: Payment frequency was assumed, not stated by a source.</li><li><b>SCHEDULE_NOT_APPROVED</b>: Original schedule is a draft awaiting review and approval.</li><li><b>ORIGINAL_SCHEDULE_MISSING</b>: Original contractual schedule (version 1) is missing.</li><li><b>SCHEDULE_INVALID</b>: Schedule has a missing date or negative cash flow.</li><li><b>SCHEDULE_DATE_INVALID</b>: A scheduled cash flow falls on or before origination.</li><li><b>SCHEDULE_DATE_DUPLICATE</b>: Schedule contains duplicate due dates.</li><li><b>PRINCIPAL_NOT_RECONCILED</b>: Scheduled principal does not reconcile to drawn within 1%.</li><li><b>FEE_CLASSIFICATION_PENDING</b>: Fee/cost lines are not independently reviewed.</li><li><b>FEE_DIRECTION_MISSING</b>: A reviewed integral fee has no cash-flow direction.</li><li><b>INITIAL_NET_INVALID</b>: Fee-adjusted initial net investment is not positive.</li></ul><p>Four newer codes are shown by their code alone: <b>TAKEON_NOT_RECOMPUTED</b> and <b>TAKEON_SOLVE_REFUSED</b> (the take-on EIR check refused the block), <b>SCHEDULE_BEFORE_FIRST_DRAWDOWN</b> (instalments fall due before any money was drawn) and <b>SCHEDULE_NOT_ON_TRANCHES</b> (the schedule was generated without the tranche register). The message for each is on the contract\'s row on Calculations.</p>'
                . '<h4>What "sole blocker" means</h4><p>The contracts for which this is the only thing holding them back. Clearing that one blocker makes exactly those contracts solvable. The highest exposure with the highest sole-blocker count is the best return on a data request.</p>'
                . '<h4>To find what to fix first</h4><ol><li>Open Blockers and choose the latest <b>Reporting period</b>.</li><li>Read the two coverage tiles.</li><li>Press the eye on the top blocker: Largest Facilities lists the largest loans it holds back.</li><li>Raise the data request for those loans, press <b>Clear blocker filter</b> and move on.</li><li>Use Coverage by Portfolio to report progress to each portfolio owner.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>What happens next</h4><p>The tabs read live data, so they move as soon as an import, an approval or a lock lands. A locked EIR is reported as the goal state, not as a blocker.</p>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li>"No blockers. Every in-scope contract can be solved."</li><li>"No in-scope contracts for this period."</li><li>"No affected facilities for these filters."</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>Coverage is 0% and every loan shows CONTRACT_PROFILE_MISSING.</b> The contract master has not been loaded, or its identifiers do not match the loan book.</li><li><b>"Not on the current tape".</b> The loan is absent from the month\'s loan book; its exposure is zero.</li><li><b>The period list is empty.</b> No loan book has been built; coverage is measured against it.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR & Revenue Recognition, EIR Calculations, Blockers.',
                'Choose the Reporting period and, if useful, a Portfolio.',
                'Read Contract coverage and Exposure coverage and the five state tiles.',
                'Press the eye on the top blocker to list the largest facilities it holds back.',
                'Raise the data request for those loans, then press Clear blocker filter and move to the next blocker.',
                'Open Coverage by Portfolio to report progress by portfolio.',
            ],
            'images' => [
                'eir-coverage' => 'Blockers: coverage by count and exposure, the state tiles and the blockers ranked by exposure',
                'eir-coverage-portfolios' => 'Coverage by Portfolio: contracts, covered and coverage per portfolio',
                'eir-coverage-facilities' => 'Largest Facilities: the largest facilities with their state and blockers',
            ],
            'routes' => ['eir-coverage.index'],
        ],

        'EIR as at a date' => [
            'body' => '<p>EIR as at a Date shows the EIR book at any date: for each contract with a locked EIR, the EIR interest and the contractual interest for the year to the date, the difference between them (the revenue shift), the amortised cost and the gross carrying amount, by product, by GL and by contract, with one contract\'s full computation on request. In the sidebar, under <b>Financial modelling</b>, open <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b>, choose <b>EIR Calculations</b> and then <b>EIR as at a Date</b> (breadcrumb: Financial modelling &gt; EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition &gt; EIR Calculations &gt; EIR as at a Date). It is also reached from the Reports hub and from <b>View as at</b> on a loan schedule page. It needs the <b>eir.view</b> permission.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li><b>Back to reports</b> (a link to the Reports hub), title <b>EIR as at a date</b>, strapline "The EIR book at any date: amortised cost, gross carrying amount, EIR and contractual interest to the date and the difference (the revenue shift)."</li>'
                . '<li>The filter bar: <b>View as at</b> (a date, at most the last posting loaded), <b>One contract (optional)</b> (<code>The whole book</code> or a contract with a locked EIR), <b>View</b>, the <b>Year-ends:</b> buttons 2024-12-31, 2025-12-31 and 2026-12-31 (greyed out when after the last posting), and "Last posting loaded: (date). A later date is refused, never estimated." The page opens at the last posting loaded.</li>'
                . '<li>Four figures: <b>Contracts with a locked EIR</b>, <b>EIR interest, year to (date)</b>, <b>Contractual interest, year to date</b> and <b>The revenue shift</b>.</li>'
                . '<li>An amber note when a month has no EIR revenue run: its EIR interest is rolled forward at the monthly EIR rather than read from a locked run ("Run the revenue for them (Maintenance, Month-end)."). A grey note counts contracts with a credit balance (the customer paid more than was owed), shown as negatives in the gross column.</li>'
                . '<li>One table, <b>The EIR book as at (date)</b>, as on the loan book. In the table itself: <b>View by</b> (<code>Contract</code>, <code>Product</code> or <code>GL</code>), <b>Product</b> (<code>All products</code> or one), <b>GL</b> (<code>All GLs</code> or one) and, by contract, <b>Contract or customer</b> (part of the number or the name). By product or by GL the rows are the contracts added up, with <b>Contracts</b>; click one to see its contracts. A Total row adds up the contracts shown ("Total of the n contract(s) shown" when a filter is set), and the table shows fifteen rows a page.</li>'
                . '<li>By contract ("Click a contract to see its full computation at this date"): <b>Contract</b>, <b>Customer</b>, <b>Product</b>, <b>GL</b>, <b>EIR YTD</b>, <b>Contractual YTD</b>, <b>Difference</b>, <b>Amortised cost</b>, <b>Gross</b> (with "credit balance" in amber where it applies). Fifteen rows a page.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>One contract\'s computation</h4><p>With a contract chosen, a panel opens above the book: the contract, customer and date (a grey <b>locked period</b> badge when the month is locked); the product, GL, account, EIR (solved and locked dates) and contractual rate; four figures, <b>Amortised cost</b> (with its basis, and for a take-on loan the take-on balance and the recomputed amortised cost), <b>Gross carrying amount</b> (its basis), <b>Difference, year to date</b> (EIR less contractual) and <b>Difference, cumulative</b> (from the first month); <b>Roll-forward</b> (<b>Period</b>, <b>Opening</b>, <b>EIR interest</b>, <b>Cash</b>, <b>Closing</b>, <b>Basis</b>, the month of the date in bold, fifteen rows a page); <b>Remaining expected cash flows (schedule vN)</b> (<b>Due</b>, <b>Principal</b>, <b>Interest</b>, <b>Fee</b>, fifteen lines a page); and <b>Modifications and solves</b> (rate resets to the date, earlier solves, and the inputs read, including the governance values).</p>'
                . '<h4>How the figures are worked out</h4><ul><li>At a month end, the roll-forward row the revenue run locked for the month.</li><li>Inside a month, the prior month-end amortised cost plus simple interest at the contractual rate for the actual days over 365, less the cash received in the month.</li><li>Months without a roll-forward row are rolled from the last closing at the monthly EIR, less the cash received.</li><li>Take-on loans open at the take-on basis: the recomputed amortised cost at 31 July 2024, or the take-on balance.</li><li>Gross carrying amount: the ledger\'s running balance of every posting on or before the date. Contractual interest: what the ledger posted (types 303 and 120).</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Buttons and icons</h4><ul><li><b>View</b>: shows the book (and the contract, if chosen) at the date.</li><li><b>Year-ends</b>: sets the date to that year end and shows it.</li><li>A contract row: opens that contract\'s computation. A product or GL row: shows its contracts.</li><li><b>CSV</b>, <b>Excel</b> and <b>Download PDF</b> (above the table, at its top right, greyed out until the book is shown): the whole book at the date, by product, by GL and by contract, with a table of how the figures are worked out. Each download needs eir.view and is written to the audit log ("EIR As At Exported").</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>To read the book at a year end</h4><ol><li>Press the year-end button, for example 2025-12-31.</li><li>Read the four figures, then set <b>View by</b> to Product or GL.</li><li>Click a contract to read its roll-forward and remaining cash flows.</li><li>Press <b>Excel</b> or <b>Download PDF</b> for the audit file.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li>"No roll-forward yet: the revenue run has not reached this contract."</li><li>A date after the last posting, or any other refusal, shows its reason in a red box above the book.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>A contract is missing from the list.</b> Only contracts with a locked EIR are in the book.</li><li><b>The amber note lists months without a run.</b> Run the month-end for those months in order (see Running the monthly revenue).</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR & Revenue Recognition, EIR Calculations, EIR as at a Date.',
                'Choose the View as at date (or press a Year-ends button) and press View.',
                'Read the four figures and the By product and By GL panels.',
                'Click a contract in By contract, or choose it in One contract, to read its full computation.',
                'Press CSV, Excel or Download PDF to keep the book at the date.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-as-at' => 'EIR as at a Date: the book by product and by GL, with the revenue shift'],
            'routes' => ['eir-as-at.index', 'eir-as-at.export'],
        ],

        'GL reconciliation' => [
            'body' => '<p>GL Reconciliation explains, for one month, why the interest income calculated at the EIR differs from the interest the general ledger posted. It sets each account\'s posting against the interest the contract charges, names the cause of every difference, and walks from the ledger to the EIR interest in effects that add up exactly. It is also where the month\'s EIR revenue can be run from the screen. In the sidebar, under <b>Financial modelling</b>, open <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b>, then <b>GL Reconciliation</b> (breadcrumb: Financial modelling &gt; EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition &gt; GL Reconciliation). Reading needs <b>eir.view</b>; the Excel and PDF downloads need <b>eir.export</b>; running the revenue needs <b>settings</b>. How the expected interest and the causes are worked out is in How the interest reconciliation works.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li><b>Back to reports</b>, title <b>GL reconciliation (EIR)</b>, strapline "Why calculated interest income differs from what the ledger posted."</li>'
                . '<li>The filter: <b>Reporting period</b> (months with GL postings, newest first), <b>Portfolio</b> (<code>All portfolios</code> or one), each applying as you change it, and <b>Apply</b>.</li>'
                . '<li>The revenue panel: <b>Amortised-cost roll-forward</b> ("n amortisation row(s) exist for (month), from m contract(s) with an approved and locked EIR. Re-running leaves calculated periods unchanged.") or, as an amber notice with an X to close it when earlier months have no rows, <b>No calculated interest to compare against</b> ("n period(s) up to (month) have no amortisation rows, starting at ..."), and "No contract has a locked EIR yet, so a run would produce nothing." when that is so.</li>'
                . '<li>Four cards: <b>Interest posted in the ledger</b>, <b>Contractual interest expected</b>, <b>Difference to explain</b> and <b>Rows agreeing with the ledger</b> ("a of b").</li>'
                . '<li>Under the four tiles, one row of tabs: <b>Variance bridge</b> (the two cards below) and <b>Facilities</b> (with the number of rows). The open tab is kept in the address. The page buttons sit under the tabs, at the right.</li>'
                . '<li>The card <b>Variance bridge, (month)</b>, with a <b>How the four effects are worked out</b> link and the lines <b>GL interest posted, all rows</b>; <b>Postings with no calculated counterpart</b> ("n row(s)"); <b>GL interest on matched facilities</b>; <b>Base effect: the ledger against the interest the contract charges</b>; <b>Contractual interest on the loan-book balance</b>; <b>Carrying amount effect: the amortised cost instead of the loan-book balance</b>; <b>Rate effect: the EIR instead of the contractual rate on the same balance</b>; <b>Impairment effect: Stage 3 accrued on net</b>; <b>Unexplained</b>; and <b>EIR interest calculated</b>. Negative amounts are in brackets.</li>'
                . '<li>The card <b>Why the ledger and the contract differ</b>: how many rows agree, are explained by a named cause or are still unexplained, and a chip per cause with its count.</li>'
                . '<li>An amber notice <b>The rate effect is effectively nil</b>, with an X to close it, when no integral fees have been classified yet.</li>'
                . '<li>On the <b>Facilities</b> tab, the card <b>Facilities</b> (sorted by absolute variance; the day count and the band in force are stated): <b>Contract</b> (customer or portfolio and the GL account beneath), <b>Basis</b> (opening balance, rate and days; the amortised balance; the balance the ledger implies; "Month of first disbursement" and "Capitalising moratorium" in amber where they apply), <b>Expected interest</b>, <b>GL posted</b> ("nothing posted", with "Expected less posted" beneath), <b>Cause</b> (badge and the explanation for the row), <b>EIR accrued</b>, <b>Variance</b> (with the percentage), <b>Variance split</b> (Carrying, Rate, Impairment) and <b>Status</b> (<b>Within tolerance</b>, <b>Variance</b>, <b>Not calculated</b>, <b>No EIR contract</b>). Fifteen rows a page.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Buttons and icons</h4><ul>'
                . '<li><b>Excel</b> and <b>Download PDF</b> (under the tabs, at the right; eir.export): the whole month with the formula, day count and band in force. Recorded in the audit trail.</li>'
                . '<li><b>GL Postings</b> (beside them): the posting list on EIR Data (see EIR data).</li>'
                . '<li><b>Run (month) only</b> (settings): runs the EIR revenue for the chosen month. Refused when an earlier month has no rows, because each opening balance is the prior month\'s closing.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Run every period to (month)</b> (settings): runs every month in order from the first up to the chosen one ("Running..." while it works). Months already calculated are left unchanged.</li>'
                . '<li>Both run buttons are greyed out while no contract has a locked EIR. Recalculating a month already run is not offered here: it needs the command with a reason (see Running the monthly revenue).</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>After a run</h4><p>A box shows "Ran n period(s): ..." with <b>Rows created</b>, <b>Already calculated</b>, <b>Blocked</b>, <b>Cash taken from the schedule</b> and <b>Unclassified cash</b>, and "Contracts that produced no row:" with each contract, month and reason ("and n more." beyond the list). A refused run shows its reason in red.</p>'
                . '<h4>The causes</h4><p><b>Agrees</b>, <b>Late disbursement</b>, <b>Catch-up posting</b>, <b>Mid-month tranche</b>, <b>Rate mismatch</b>, <b>Nothing posted</b>, <b>Data gap</b> and <b>Unexplained</b>; each is explained, with what to do, in How the interest reconciliation works.</p>'
                . '<h4>To work a reconciliation</h4><ol><li>Make sure the month\'s trial balance or Extract C is loaded and the revenue has been run (press <b>Run every period to (month)</b> if the panel is amber).</li><li>Choose the <b>Reporting period</b> and, if useful, a <b>Portfolio</b>.</li><li>Read the four cards and the bridge; the footer says whether anything is unexplained.</li><li>Work down Facilities from the largest variance and read the cause on each row.</li><li>Press <b>Excel</b> or <b>Download PDF</b> for the audit file.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li>"No GL interest postings have been loaded yet. Use EIR Data Intake to load an interest posting extract."</li><li>"No GL postings or live loan-book rows for this period and portfolio."</li><li>Bridge footers: "No EIR interest is calculated for this month yet, so nothing is reconciled: the whole posting is unmatched. Run the EIR revenue for the month first.", "The four effects account for the variance in full: no unexplained residual." or "(amount) is not explained by any of the four effects. ..."</li><li>"There are no GL interest postings loaded yet, so there is nothing to export."</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>Every row says Not calculated.</b> The revenue has not been run for the month, or the loans are not locked.</li><li><b>Rows say No EIR contract.</b> The contract master has not been loaded for those loans, or the identifiers differ.</li><li><b>The rate effect is nil.</b> No fee is yet reviewed as integral, so the EIR equals the contractual rate.</li><li><b>The run buttons answer with a rights error.</b> Running the revenue needs settings.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR & Revenue Recognition, GL Reconciliation.',
                'Choose the Reporting period and, if useful, the Portfolio.',
                'If the revenue panel is amber, press Run every period to the month (needs settings) and read the result box.',
                'Read the four cards and the variance bridge.',
                'Open the Facilities tab, work down the table from the largest variance and read the cause on each row.',
                'Press Excel or Download PDF to keep the month for the audit file.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-reconciliation' => 'GL reconciliation (EIR): the revenue panel, the four cards and the variance bridge'],
            'routes' => ['eir-reconciliation.index', 'eir-reconciliation.run-revenue', 'eir-reconciliation.export'],
        ],

        'How the interest reconciliation works' => [
            'body' => '<p>Two figures exist for the same loan in the same month: the interest the core banking system posted to the general ledger, and the interest the loan contract says should have been charged. The reconciliation on <b>EIR Calculations</b>, <b>GL Reconciliation</b> puts them side by side, account by account, and names the reason for every difference. This article explains the method; the screen itself is described in GL reconciliation.</p>'
                . '<h4>The formula</h4>'
                . '<p>The expected interest for a month is the <b>outstanding balance at the end of the month before, times the annual interest rate, times the number of days in the calendar month, divided by 365</b>. It is the calculation that reproduces the bank\'s own postings to the tambala, proven on MAIIC\'s loan books and general ledger before it was built.</p>'
                . '<p>Three rules go with it:</p>'
                . '<ul>'
                . '<li><b>The month the money is paid out.</b> Interest runs from the disbursement day itself to the end of the month. Money paid out on 8 August is charged 24 days of August.</li>'
                . '<li><b>A moratorium that defers interest as well as capital.</b> The interest is added to the balance every month, so the next month opens on a balance higher by exactly the interest charged.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Never the annual rate divided by twelve.</b> In February a twelfth overstates the charge by between 8 and 9 percent.</li>'
                . '</ul>'
                . '<h4>Where each input comes from</h4>'
                . '<p>The opening balance is the outstanding balance on the previous month\'s Loan Book Report. The rate is the one the loan book carries for the month; where it carries none, the contract master is used, and the row says which. The day count and the band inside which a difference is treated as agreement are Governance Centre settings (<b>Day count</b> and <b>When a difference is an exception</b>), read for the month being reconciled, so a month already reported keeps the basis it was calculated on. Where an input is missing the engine produces no figure: the row says what is missing.</p>'
                . '<h4>The cause on each row, and what to do about it</h4>'
                . '<ul>'
                . '<li><b>Agrees.</b> Inside the governed band. Nothing to do.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Late disbursement.</b> The loan was paid out during the month. If the ledger started a day later than the disbursement date, the date held may be wrong.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Catch-up posting.</b> The ledger posted earlier months all at once; the row names the months it covers. Read them together.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Mid-month tranche.</b> More money was drawn during the month. Load the drawdowns (EIR Data, Drawdowns) so the days can be counted.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Rate mismatch.</b> The posting works out at a rate another record carries. Ask MAIIC which rate the core system holds, and whether the loan was repriced.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Nothing posted.</b> The loan is live with a balance and the ledger has no interest for it. Ask for the posting or the reason (litigation or suspense is a valid answer).</li>'
                . '<li><b>Data gap.</b> Something the calculation needs is missing: no loan book for the month before, no rate, no disbursement date. Load it, then look again.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Unexplained.</b> None of the causes accounts for the difference. The row shows the rate the posting implies. Clear or explain each in writing before the period is closed.</li>'
                . '</ul>'
                . '<h4>The bridge</h4>'
                . '<p>The bridge walks from what the ledger posted to the interest calculated at the EIR in four effects that add up exactly. The <b>base effect</b> is the ledger against the contract, the total of the differences the causes explain. The <b>carrying amount effect</b> is the same charge on the balance the engine amortises rather than the loan-book balance. The <b>rate effect</b> is accruing at the EIR instead of the contractual rate on the same balance, which is where integral fees show up. The <b>impairment effect</b> is a Stage 3 loan accruing on the amount net of its loss allowance, correct under IFRS 9. Anything the four cannot account for is shown as unexplained. Postings with no calculated counterpart are kept out of the bridge, because a missing row is a coverage gap, not a measurement difference.</p>'
                . '<h4>The downloads</h4>'
                . '<p><b>Excel</b> and <b>Download PDF</b> give the whole month: the formula, the day count and the band in force, one row per account with its opening balance, rate, days, expected interest, posted interest, difference and cause, and what each cause means. Both need the <b>eir.export</b> permission, and every download is recorded in the audit trail with the settings it was produced under.</p>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR & Revenue Recognition, GL Reconciliation.',
                'Choose the reporting period and, if you want a narrower view, the portfolio.',
                'Read the four cards: interest posted, contractual interest expected, the difference to explain and the rows that agree.',
                'Work down the facilities table from the largest difference; the Cause column names the reason.',
                'Clear every row whose cause asks something of you: a missing month, rate, drawdown or posting.',
                'Investigate anything marked Unexplained and write down the conclusion before the period is closed.',
                'Press Excel or Download PDF to keep the month, with the settings it was worked out under.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-reconciliation' => 'The facilities table with the expected interest and the cause on each row'],
        ],

        'Running the monthly revenue' => [
            'body' => '<p>The monthly revenue run rolls every locked loan forward one month at its EIR: opening balance, interest accrued, cash received, closing balance. Its rows are the calculated interest GL Reconciliation compares with the ledger and the amortised cost EIR as at a Date reports. It is one step of the month-end, run after the month\'s loan book is built and approved. There are three ways to run it: the month-end job on <b>Administration &amp; support</b>, <b>Support &amp; Maintenance</b>, <b>Maintenance</b>, <b>Month-end</b> (needs <b>settings</b>); the run buttons on <b>EIR Calculations</b>, <b>GL Reconciliation</b> (needs <b>settings</b>); and the command line.</p>'
                . '<h4>The month-end, in order</h4><p>For the month being closed (September 2026 in the examples), as in the Installation Guide, section 9.9:</p><ol>'
                . '<li>Land the E-Banker pack (Imports &amp; Feeds, E-Banker Loads; <code>php artisan eir:land-pack</code>).</li>'
                . '<li>Build the month\'s loan book; a second person approves it (Imports &amp; Feeds, Loan Book Builds; <code>php artisan eir:build-loan-books 2026-09</code>).</li>'
                . '<li>Load the trial balance (EIR Data, Trial Balances) and any change to the prime lending rate (EIR Data, Reference Rates).</li>'
                . '<li>Raise or close qualitative SICR triggers for the month.</li>'
                . '<li>Run the month-end calculations: Maintenance, Month-end, <b>Run the month-end calculations</b>, or <code>php artisan maiic:task month-end 2026-09 --user=ID</code>. In order: the scheme settings, the restructure candidates and rate moves (detection only: nothing is confirmed until two people do so on Restructures &amp; Resets), staging, PD and LGD by segment, the forward-looking route and the ECL, the actual cash from the E-Banker ledger, the GL fee postings with the fee GL check, <b>the EIR revenue</b>, then the Mega Farm programme. It stops at the first step that cannot run and says why.</li>'
                . '<li>Confirm any restructure, rate reset or write-off of the month, and re-run the revenue from its month (below).</li>'
                . '<li>Review GL Reconciliation and EIR as at a Date, then the Workspace close checklist.</li>'
                . '<li>Sign the month off and lock it: <code>php artisan eir:lock-period 2026-09 --user=ID --reason="September 2026 close"</code>. No later run restates a locked month.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>The command</h4><p><code>php artisan eir:run-revenue 2026-09 --user=ID</code> runs the month for every locked contract. <code>--user</code> is required: the run is recorded against the person who ran it. Options: <code>--contract=ID</code> (repeatable) limits the run to named loans; <code>--queue</code> puts it on the queue; <code>--recalculate</code> replaces rows already held for the month and needs <code>--reason="..."</code>.</p>'
                . '<h4>What the run needs</h4><ul><li>A locked EIR: otherwise "The original EIR must be approved and locked before revenue can be run."</li><li>A loan-book row for the loan in that month: otherwise "No loan-book snapshot exists for (month)."</li><li>A valid IFRS 9 stage (1, 2 or 3): otherwise "The IFRS 9 stage for (month) is missing or invalid."</li><li>An open month: a locked month is refused for every contract with the locked-period message.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>What it produces</h4><p>One amortisation row per loan per month: <b>opening gross</b> (the previous closing; for the first month the initial net investment, or for a take-on loan the balance at 31 July 2024, the roll-forward then starting in August 2024); <b>interest accrued</b> at the monthly EIR; <b>interest basis</b> GROSS for Stage 1 and 2, NET for Stage 3 (on the balance net of the allowance); the <b>unwind</b>; <b>cash received</b> and its <b>source</b>; any modification gain or loss booked from a confirmed restructure; <b>closing gross</b>; and the <b>ECL allowance</b> used. Cash comes from the loan\'s actual transactions (the E-Banker ledger and Extract B actual rows) where the feed covers the month: collections count as cash (source IMPORTED); a covered month with no receipt is nil cash; a drawdown is money advanced, never netted off; a write-off is derecognition, not cash; any other type is reported as unclassified cash. Where the feed does not cover the month, the schedule\'s due amounts are used and the row is labelled DERIVED.</p>'
                . '<h4>What the terminal shows</h4><p>"EIR revenue run completed for (month)." and a table with <b>Requested</b>, <b>Created</b>, <b>Recalculated</b>, <b>Unchanged</b>, <b>Blocked</b>, <b>Rows superseded</b>, <b>Cash from schedule</b> and <b>Unclassified cash</b>, then "n contract(s) produced no amortisation row:" with each contract and reason. An audit entry "EIR Revenue Run Completed" (or "EIR Revenue Recalculated") stores the summary.</p>'
                . '<h4>Recalculating a month</h4><p>A row already held is left alone and counted as Unchanged. To replace it, pass both <code>--recalculate</code> and <code>--reason</code>; without the reason the command refuses: "--recalculate requires --reason: a restated figure must carry its justification." Each opening balance is the previous closing, so restating one month voids every later month for the loans affected: "Recalculating (month) also voids every later period for each affected contract ... Re-run those periods in order afterwards." The replaced rows are kept in history with the reason. The screen buttons never recalculate.</p>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>"Period must use YYYY-MM format."</b></li><li><b>"--user=&lt;id&gt; is required ..."</b> Give your user id.</li><li><b>"The locked EIR snapshot has no positive initial net investment."</b> Reopen, correct the fees and solve again.</li><li><b>"No remaining contractual cash flows exist after (month)."</b> The schedule ends before the month.</li><li><b>Unchanged is high.</b> The month was already run; recalculate only if the inputs genuinely changed.</li><li><b>Cash from schedule is high.</b> The ledger feed does not cover the month for those loans; land the pack before relying on the figures.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Land the month\'s E-Banker pack and have the loan book build approved by a second person.',
                'Load the trial balance and any change to the prime lending rate.',
                'Run the month-end calculations on Maintenance, Month-end (or php artisan maiic:task month-end YYYY-MM --user=ID); the EIR revenue is one of its steps.',
                'Read the result; resolve each blocked contract using the reason shown.',
                'After confirming a restructure or write-off, re-run the revenue from its month with php artisan eir:run-revenue YYYY-MM --user=ID --recalculate --reason="...", then every later month in order.',
                'Review GL Reconciliation and EIR as at a Date for the month.',
                'Sign the month off and lock it with eir:lock-period.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-reconciliation' => 'GL Reconciliation, where the month\'s revenue rows are read and can be run'],
        ],

        'Governance Centre' => [
            'body' => '<p>The Governance Centre keeps every convention the EIR and forward-looking engines calculate with: the day count, the reconciliation band, the derecognition test, who may approve a schedule, the administrator override and many more. Each setting has a fixed list of options, a value in force, the date it took effect, and who proposed and approved it. In the sidebar, under <b>Administration &amp; support</b>, open <b>Governance</b> and choose <b>Governance Centre</b>. It needs the <b>eir.govern</b> permission; users without it do not see the entry. The Administrator Manual describes the page in full; this article covers what a user of the EIR screens needs to know.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see</h4><ul><li>Title <b>Governance Centre</b> and the rule: "A change is proposed by one person and applies from its effective date forward once a second person approves it. A month already run keeps the settings it ran with; a setting with no approved value stops the calculation that needs it rather than guessing."</li><li>Two views, <b>Settings by area</b> and <b>Needs attention</b> (changes awaiting approval, approved changes that start later, settings with no approved value). When there is none, a green notice with an X to close it: "Nothing needs attention: every setting has an approved value and no change is waiting."</li><li>A card per setting with its value in force, or in red "No approved value: the calculation stops". Choosing a card opens its options, every value it has had and its superseded values (fifteen rows a page each), <b>Propose a change</b> and <b>Close</b>.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>"Recorded, not yet applied"</h4><p>A grey badge <b>recorded, not yet applied</b> on a setting means no engine reads it yet: the value is kept and governed, but changing it changes no figure. Hover over the badge, or open the setting, for the note on why and what it waits for. Settings without the badge are read by the engines.</p>'
                . '<h4>Who can change a setting</h4><p>A user with <b>eir.govern</b> presses <b>Propose a change</b>, chooses the option, sets the effective date (later than the last approved change) and gives a reason of at least 10 characters. A second person with eir.govern presses <b>Approve</b> on the proposal; the proposer cannot ("The person who proposed a change cannot approve it"), unless <b>Administrator override of maker-checker</b> is <b>Allowed for administrators</b> and they hold the administrator role. Every change is in the audit log with the old and new value.</p>'
                . '<h4>Settings the EIR screens read</h4><ul><li><b>Approving a version 1 schedule</b>: whether a second person must approve (Schedule Review).</li><li><b>Administrator override of maker-checker</b>: whether an administrator may approve their own work and reopen a locked EIR (Calculations, Fee Classification).</li><li><b>Interest basis on a partly drawn facility</b> (schedule generation), <b>The derecognition test</b> and <b>Reset or modification</b> (Restructures &amp; Resets), <b>Day count</b> and <b>When a difference is an exception</b> (GL Reconciliation), <b>Fee imports checked against the fee GLs</b> and <b>Which source a contract\'s fees come from</b> (Fee Classification), and <b>Take-on EIR: the check before it is used</b> (take-on loans).</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Why a locked month keeps its settings</h4><p>The engine reads the value in force on the last day of the month it is calculating, not today\'s, so a month already run is repeatable and the old value stays on the page as history.</p>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open Administration & support, Governance, Governance Centre (needs eir.govern).',
                'Read the value in force for the setting; a red line means no value is approved and the calculation that needs it stops.',
                'A grey "recorded, not yet applied" badge means no engine reads the setting yet.',
                'To change a setting, open it, press Propose a change, choose the option, set the effective date and give the reason.',
                'Ask a second person with eir.govern to press Approve on the proposal.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-governance' => 'The Governance Centre: settings by area with the value in force'],
            'routes' => ['eir-governance.index'],
        ],
    ],
];
